window.BOOK_DATA = {"title":"Agriculture Grade 9 Learner's Book","intro":["Agriculture","Grade 9","Learner's Book","Table of Contents","1.0 Conservation of Resources ........ 1","1.1 Conserving Animal Feed: Hay ........ 1","1.2 Conserving Leftover Foods ........ 9","1.3 Integrated Farming ........ 18","2.0 Food Production Processes ........ 23","2.1 Organic Gardening ........ 23","2.2 Storage of Crop Produce ........ 42","2.3 Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures ........ 47","3.0 Hygiene Practices ........ 55","3.1 Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities ........ 55","3.2 Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles ........ 63","4.0 Production Techniques ........ 72","4.1 Grafting in Plants ........ 72","4.2 Homemade Sun Dryer ........ 76"],"strands":[{"code":"1.0","title":"Conservation of Resources","substrands":[{"code":"1.1","title":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","pages":[1,8]},{"code":"1.2","title":"Conserving Leftover Foods","pages":[9,17]},{"code":"1.3","title":"Integrated Farming","pages":[18,22]}]},{"code":"2.0","title":"Food Production Processes","substrands":[{"code":"2.1","title":"Organic Gardening","pages":[23,41]},{"code":"2.2","title":"Storage of Crop Produce","pages":[42,46]},{"code":"2.3","title":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","pages":[47,54]}]},{"code":"3.0","title":"Hygiene Practices","substrands":[{"code":"3.1","title":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","pages":[55,62]},{"code":"3.2","title":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","pages":[63,71]}]},{"code":"4.0","title":"Production Techniques","substrands":[{"code":"4.1","title":"Grafting in Plants","pages":[72,75]},{"code":"4.2","title":"Homemade Sun Dryer","pages":[76,79]}]}],"pages":[{"page":1,"pdfPage":5,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"strand","text":"Conservation of Resources","code":"1.0"},{"t":"p","html":"In this strand, we will learn how to conserve forage as hay so as to solve the problem of scarce animal feeds during drought seasons. We will also learn how to prepare leftover foods in our homes for consumption to avoid wastage. Finally, we will learn how to recycle resources within the farm through integrated farming to reduce costs.","indent":false},{"t":"substrand","text":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","code":"1.1"},{"t":"p","html":"During long and short rainy seasons, there is plenty of forage crops (pastures and fodder crops) for domestic animals in most parts of the country. However, during drought seasons, there is scarcity of forage crops leading to starvation and death of the animals. In pastoralist areas, farmers migrate their animals to faraway places in search of pasture. This sometimes leads to communal conflicts as animals compete to graze on limited pastures. Learning how to conserve excess forage can cushion households from fodder shortages during dry seasons. We will learn how to conserve forage through making hay.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Methods of conserving forage"},{"t":"p","html":"Forage are plants which grow naturally or are planted for feeding livestock animals. The forage plants which are grazed directly by livestock are called <strong>pastures</strong> while those which are harvested and then fed to livestock are called <strong>fodder crops</strong>. We will focus on methods of conserving forage as hay, namely baled hay, stacking hay and standing hay.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What is hay?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"What forage crops are suitable for making hay?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Which animals feed on hay?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas and experiences on how hay is prepared."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Hay refers to forage that has been cut and then dried to about 15 to 20% moisture content. Grasses make good hay. However, the type of forage material should be considered when selecting the forage for hay making. For example, grasses with thin stems are preferred to those with thick stems. Grasses such as napier grass may not make good hay because their thick stems may not lose moisture easily unlike Boma Rhodes grass.","indent":false,"continues":true}],"text":"Conservation of Resources\nIn this strand, we will learn how to conserve forage as hay so as to solve the problem of scarce animal feeds during drought seasons. We will also learn how to prepare leftover foods in our homes for consumption to avoid wastage. Finally, we will learn how to recycle resources within the farm through integrated farming to reduce costs.\nConserving Animal Feed: Hay\nDuring long and short rainy seasons, there is plenty of forage crops (pastures and fodder crops) for domestic animals in most parts of the country. However, during drought seasons, there is scarcity of forage crops leading to starvation and death of the animals. In pastoralist areas, farmers migrate their animals to faraway places in search of pasture. This sometimes leads to communal conflicts as animals compete to graze on limited pastures. Learning how to conserve excess forage can cushion households from fodder shortages during dry seasons. We will learn how to conserve forage through making hay.\nMethods of conserving forage\nForage are plants which grow naturally or are planted for feeding livestock animals. The forage plants which are grazed directly by livestock are called pastures while those which are harvested and then fed to livestock are called fodder crops. We will focus on methods of conserving forage as hay, namely baled hay, stacking hay and standing hay.\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. What is hay?\n2. What forage crops are suitable for making hay?\n3. Which animals feed on hay?\n4. Share your ideas and experiences on how hay is prepared.\nLet us learn\nHay refers to forage that has been cut and then dried to about 15 to 20% moisture content. Grasses make good hay. However, the type of forage material should be considered when selecting the forage for hay making. For example, grasses with thin stems are preferred to those with thick stems. Grasses such as napier grass may not make good hay because their thick stems may not lose moisture easily unlike Boma Rhodes grass."},{"page":2,"pdfPage":6,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"Other forage crops that may be used as hay include lucerne, desmodium, Sudan grass, forage sorghum, pearl millet, oat, barley and rice straws.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p02-1.jpg","w":826,"h":430,"caption":"Pearl millet; Boma Rhodes; Sudan grass","labels":""},{"t":"h4","text":"A. Preparing and baling hay"},{"t":"p","html":"How is hay prepared for baling?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Using a digital device, search for information or video clips on how to prepare baled hay for storage. Your search should focus on the following:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Suitable forage crops for hay."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Best stage for harvesting the forage."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Best weather conditions when harvesting forage and preparing it for hay."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Curing process (preparation) after cutting the forage crop to maintain quality of hay."},{"n":"e)","level":2,"html":"How to bale hay."},{"n":"f)","level":2,"html":"How to store the hay to reduce loss of nutrients."},{"n":"g)","level":2,"html":"Factors that affect quality of hay."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Alternatively, seek the information from a resource person who understands how hay is prepared."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Record important points on hay making and discuss with the rest of the class."}]}],"continuedFrom":1,"text":"Other forage crops that may be used as hay include lucerne, desmodium, Sudan grass, forage sorghum, pearl millet, oat, barley and rice straws.\nPearl millet; Boma Rhodes; Sudan grass\nA. Preparing and baling hay\nHow is hay prepared for baling?\nActivity 2 Let us go digital\n1. Using a digital device, search for information or video clips on how to prepare baled hay for storage. Your search should focus on the following:\na) Suitable forage crops for hay.\nb) Best stage for harvesting the forage.\nc) Best weather conditions when harvesting forage and preparing it for hay.\nd) Curing process (preparation) after cutting the forage crop to maintain quality of hay.\ne) How to bale hay.\nf) How to store the hay to reduce loss of nutrients.\ng) Factors that affect quality of hay.\n2. Alternatively, seek the information from a resource person who understands how hay is prepared.\n3. Record important points on hay making and discuss with the rest of the class."},{"page":3,"pdfPage":7,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Forage crops for hay are harvested at the flowering stage because that is when concentration of nutrients is at the highest. The weather during the harvesting and preparation stage should be hot and dry.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"When harvesting manually, a sickle or scythe is used to cut the forage.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p03-1.jpg","w":435,"h":461,"caption":"Harvesting forage for hay making","labels":""},{"t":"p","html":"The cut forage is left to sun dry (wilt) for 2 to 3 days depending on the prevailing weather conditions. Within that period, the forage is spread evenly or turned using a rake a few times to ensure uniform drying. The forage at that point should not break easily.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p03-2.jpg","w":435,"h":472,"caption":"Spreading hay to ensure uniform drying","labels":""}],"text":"Let us learn\nForage crops for hay are harvested at the flowering stage because that is when concentration of nutrients is at the highest. The weather during the harvesting and preparation stage should be hot and dry.\nWhen harvesting manually, a sickle or scythe is used to cut the forage.\nHarvesting forage for hay making\nThe cut forage is left to sun dry (wilt) for 2 to 3 days depending on the prevailing weather conditions. Within that period, the forage is spread evenly or turned using a rake a few times to ensure uniform drying. The forage at that point should not break easily.\nSpreading hay to ensure uniform drying"},{"page":4,"pdfPage":8,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"Dried hay should be gathered and put in a wooden box measuring 75 cm by 50 cm by 40 cm and compressed manually.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p04-1.jpg","w":435,"h":462,"caption":"Compacting hay","labels":""},{"t":"p","html":"The hay should be tied with sisal or synthetic twine and then stored in bales in a raised and well aerated shed away from rain and water.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p04-2.jpg","w":725,"h":556,"caption":"Baled hay in a shed","labels":""},{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Note:</strong> Forage planted on small scale can also be cut using hand held mowers instead of sickles. However, forage planted on large scale is harvested using tractor drawn mowers and baled using machines.","indent":false}],"text":"Dried hay should be gathered and put in a wooden box measuring 75 cm by 50 cm by 40 cm and compressed manually.\nCompacting hay\nThe hay should be tied with sisal or synthetic twine and then stored in bales in a raised and well aerated shed away from rain and water.\nBaled hay in a shed\nNote: Forage planted on small scale can also be cut using hand held mowers instead of sickles. However, forage planted on large scale is harvested using tractor drawn mowers and baled using machines."},{"page":5,"pdfPage":9,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"h4","text":"B. Conserving crop residues by stacking method"},{"t":"p","html":"After harvesting crops, crop remains such as straws and stovers can be conserved for consumption by some livestock animals. How are such crop residues conserved?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"During a visit to a farm, Grade 9 learners observed some stovers of maize stored by stacking method after the maize grains had been harvested.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p05-1.jpg","w":529,"h":426,"caption":"Stacked maize stovers","labels":""},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which other forage crops are given to livestock as dry crop residues?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Why should the stacked stovers have a conical shape at the top?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"What precautions should be taken to ensure the stovers do not lose their nutritive value?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Compare the quality of stovers with baled hay."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Share with the rest of the class."}]},{"t":"h4","text":"C. Standing forage or hay"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What is standing hay?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Why is standing hay important?"}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"While searching on various methods of conserving hay, Grade 9 learners learnt that a piece of land can be planted with forage crops and left aside for the forage to grow.","indent":false}],"text":"B. Conserving crop residues by stacking method\nAfter harvesting crops, crop remains such as straws and stovers can be conserved for consumption by some livestock animals. How are such crop residues conserved?\nActivity 3 Let us talk (in pairs)\nDuring a visit to a farm, Grade 9 learners observed some stovers of maize stored by stacking method after the maize grains had been harvested.\nStacked maize stovers\n1. Which other forage crops are given to livestock as dry crop residues?\n2. Why should the stacked stovers have a conical shape at the top?\n3. What precautions should be taken to ensure the stovers do not lose their nutritive value?\n4. Compare the quality of stovers with baled hay.\n5. Share with the rest of the class.\nC. Standing forage or hay\n1. What is standing hay?\n2. Why is standing hay important?\nActivity 4 Let us talk (in pairs)\nWhile searching on various methods of conserving hay, Grade 9 learners learnt that a piece of land can be planted with forage crops and left aside for the forage to grow."},{"page":6,"pdfPage":10,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"During the dry season, animals are allowed to graze on the forage. Such method of forage conservation is called standing hay.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p06-1.jpg","w":721,"h":459,"caption":"Cattle grazing on standing hay or forage","labels":""},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Suggest why the method of conservation described above is called standing hay."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Suggest one advantage of standing hay."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Suggest one disadvantage of such a method of forage conservation when compared to the previous methods that you have learned so far (Baled hay and stacked hay)."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas with the rest of the class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Project on forage conservation (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify where to obtain forage crops for conservation in your locality."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Decide which method of forage conservation (baled hay or stacked hay) will be suitable for the forage crops that are available in your locality."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"List all the tools, equipment and materials that you will require and discuss with your teacher how they will be availed."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Harvest and conserve forage of your choice using any of the two methods. <strong>Note:</strong> Since this project is meant to showcase the skills learnt so far, each group is required to conserve a small amount of forage (about 5 to 10 kg in weight)."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"In your journal, record your experiences and any lessons learnt while carrying out the project."}]}],"text":"During the dry season, animals are allowed to graze on the forage. Such method of forage conservation is called standing hay.\nCattle grazing on standing hay or forage\n1. Suggest why the method of conservation described above is called standing hay.\n2. Suggest one advantage of standing hay.\n3. Suggest one disadvantage of such a method of forage conservation when compared to the previous methods that you have learned so far (Baled hay and stacked hay).\n4. Share your ideas with the rest of the class.\nActivity 5 Project on forage conservation (in groups)\n1. Identify where to obtain forage crops for conservation in your locality.\n2. Decide which method of forage conservation (baled hay or stacked hay) will be suitable for the forage crops that are available in your locality.\n3. List all the tools, equipment and materials that you will require and discuss with your teacher how they will be availed.\n4. Harvest and conserve forage of your choice using any of the two methods. Note: Since this project is meant to showcase the skills learnt so far, each group is required to conserve a small amount of forage (about 5 to 10 kg in weight).\n5. In your journal, record your experiences and any lessons learnt while carrying out the project."},{"page":7,"pdfPage":11,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Discuss the importance of conserving forage at household level."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Discuss how to ensure that hay is of good quality."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Write your suggestions on a manila sheet and present your work during gallery walk."}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Take Home Activity"},{"t":"p","html":"Use the knowledge and skills acquired to discuss with your parent or guardian how to conserve hay for feeding animals such as sheep, goats, cattle and rabbits.","indent":false},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Forage can be preserved as baled hay, stacked stovers or standing hay. Conservation of forage helps to:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"Provide feed to animals during the dry season."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"Ensure forage is well distributed throughout the year."},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"Ensure better utilisation of available land."},{"n":"d.","level":2,"html":"Provide income to households when excess hay is sold."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Baled hay is of higher quality in terms of nutritive value compared to stovers and standing hay. The quality of hay is determined by factors such as:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"The type of forage which is harvested. For example, grasses with thin stems are of high quality."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"Stage of harvesting. When grasses are at the flowering stage, the quality of forage is high since nutrients are at high concentration."},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"How well the cut pasture or fodder is dried."},{"n":"d.","level":2,"html":"Length of the drying period. Prolonged drying lowers the quality of hay since nutrients are lost through breakdown of chlorophyll and carotene. This is shown by bleaching of hay. High moisture content of hay at the time of storage can lead to fermentation and growth of mould, hence loss of nutrients."},{"n":"e.","level":2,"html":"Weather conditions during harvesting and drying period. There should be no rain."},{"n":"f.","level":2,"html":"Condition of the storage structure. Hay should be kept in a cool, dry, well ventilated and roofed structure."},{"n":"g.","level":2,"html":"Inclusion of legume pasture such as lucerne or desmodium in the grass pasture. Legume pastures have more protein than grass pastures."}]}],"text":"Activity 6 Let us talk (in groups)\n1. Discuss the importance of conserving forage at household level.\n2. Discuss how to ensure that hay is of good quality.\n3. Write your suggestions on a manila sheet and present your work during gallery walk.\nTake Home Activity\nUse the knowledge and skills acquired to discuss with your parent or guardian how to conserve hay for feeding animals such as sheep, goats, cattle and rabbits.\nLet us learn\nForage can be preserved as baled hay, stacked stovers or standing hay. Conservation of forage helps to:\na. Provide feed to animals during the dry season.\nb. Ensure forage is well distributed throughout the year.\nc. Ensure better utilisation of available land.\nd. Provide income to households when excess hay is sold.\nBaled hay is of higher quality in terms of nutritive value compared to stovers and standing hay. The quality of hay is determined by factors such as:\na. The type of forage which is harvested. For example, grasses with thin stems are of high quality.\nb. Stage of harvesting. When grasses are at the flowering stage, the quality of forage is high since nutrients are at high concentration.\nc. How well the cut pasture or fodder is dried.\nd. Length of the drying period. Prolonged drying lowers the quality of hay since nutrients are lost through breakdown of chlorophyll and carotene. This is shown by bleaching of hay. High moisture content of hay at the time of storage can lead to fermentation and growth of mould, hence loss of nutrients.\ne. Weather conditions during harvesting and drying period. There should be no rain.\nf. Condition of the storage structure. Hay should be kept in a cool, dry, well ventilated and roofed structure.\ng. Inclusion of legume pasture such as lucerne or desmodium in the grass pasture. Legume pastures have more protein than grass pastures."},{"page":8,"pdfPage":12,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.1","substrandTitle":"Conserving Animal Feed: Hay","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":""},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"You have been given a task of harvesting Boma Rhodes grass which has been planted on a small plot of land. You are also required to conserve it as hay in bales. Describe how you will carry out the assigned task."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Describe other ways of conserving the fodder."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"A Grade 9 learner observed that some stored hay had moulds. After some time, the hay was rotten and therefore unfit for feeding the cattle."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Suggest possible reasons why the hay was in that situation."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Give other factors that may affect the quality of hay."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Why should farmers conserve forage?"}]}],"text":"Assess yourself\n1.\na) You have been given a task of harvesting Boma Rhodes grass which has been planted on a small plot of land. You are also required to conserve it as hay in bales. Describe how you will carry out the assigned task.\nb) Describe other ways of conserving the fodder.\n2. A Grade 9 learner observed that some stored hay had moulds. After some time, the hay was rotten and therefore unfit for feeding the cattle.\na) Suggest possible reasons why the hay was in that situation.\nb) Give other factors that may affect the quality of hay.\n3. Why should farmers conserve forage?"},{"page":9,"pdfPage":13,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Conserving Leftover Foods","code":"1.2"},{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of conserving leftover foods at home"},{"t":"p","html":"Despite our best efforts in meal planning, having leftover food after meals is inevitable. This can lead to food wastage. Therefore, it is important to manage leftover foods effectively to avoid wastage of food.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Have you ever had leftover food after taking a meal?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Share your experiences on what you did with the leftover food."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Study the pictures below and answer the questions that follow.</strong>","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p09-1.jpg","w":810,"h":587,"caption":"","labels":"Let us make ugali toast for breakfast using this leftover ugali. Please take this food so that you and your family can have it for supper. We have a lot of leftover food from the party."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Describe how each of the ways shown in the pictures help to conserve leftover foods."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Brainstorm on hygiene practices that should be observed when handling leftover foods."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class."}]}],"text":"Conserving Leftover Foods\nImportance of conserving leftover foods at home\nDespite our best efforts in meal planning, having leftover food after meals is inevitable. This can lead to food wastage. Therefore, it is important to manage leftover foods effectively to avoid wastage of food.\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. Have you ever had leftover food after taking a meal?\n2. Share your experiences on what you did with the leftover food.\nActivity 2 Let us talk (in groups)\nStudy the pictures below and answer the questions that follow.\nLet us make ugali toast for breakfast using this leftover ugali. Please take this food so that you and your family can have it for supper. We have a lot of leftover food from the party.\n1. Describe how each of the ways shown in the pictures help to conserve leftover foods.\n2. Brainstorm on hygiene practices that should be observed when handling leftover foods.\n3. Share your ideas in class."},{"page":10,"pdfPage":14,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Conserving leftover foods means taking steps to avoid wasting extra food after a meal. This involves storing leftover foods properly, sharing them with friends or relatives and using creative ways to include them in new meals.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover foods should be stored in clean containers that are covered well, then stored in a refrigerator or cool place. This is to avoid food contamination which may lead to food poisoning.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Read the following story and answer the questions that follow.</strong>","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"One Sunday, Susan, a Grade 9 learner, was left at home with her two younger siblings since their parents had gone to visit their grandparents. When it was time for lunch, her younger siblings began to express their hunger, asking Susan for something to eat. Susan remembered there were leftovers in the fridge from the previous night's dinner; rice, stewed beef and vegetables. Instead of ordering for fast foods, she saw this as an opportunity to turn the leftovers into a delicious homemade meal.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Susan heated the leftover rice and beef in a pan and added soy sauce for flavour. Next, she tossed in the vegetables, creating an aromatic stir fry. Once the meal was ready, Susan served her siblings. To her delight, her siblings loved the meal. This proved that with creativity, leftovers can be turned into a delicious and satisfying meal.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which values did Susan's actions teach her younger siblings?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"From the story, what are the benefits of turning leftovers into a new dish?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Explain other benefits of conserving leftover foods at home."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"The following are the benefits of conserving leftover meals at home:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Prevents wastage of food: Reheating dishes allows one to eat leftover food which ensures that there is no wastage of food."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Saves money: This is because a meal can be produced without buying ingredients."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Saves energy: Reheating dishes does not use a lot of fuel."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Saves labour: Since reheating dishes requires little to no preparation, it saves the effort that would have been used for normal food preparation and cooking."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Saves time: Reheating dishes is faster compared to cooking a meal."}]}],"text":"Let us learn\nConserving leftover foods means taking steps to avoid wasting extra food after a meal. This involves storing leftover foods properly, sharing them with friends or relatives and using creative ways to include them in new meals.\nLeftover foods should be stored in clean containers that are covered well, then stored in a refrigerator or cool place. This is to avoid food contamination which may lead to food poisoning.\nActivity 3 Let us talk (in groups)\nRead the following story and answer the questions that follow.\nOne Sunday, Susan, a Grade 9 learner, was left at home with her two younger siblings since their parents had gone to visit their grandparents. When it was time for lunch, her younger siblings began to express their hunger, asking Susan for something to eat. Susan remembered there were leftovers in the fridge from the previous night's dinner; rice, stewed beef and vegetables. Instead of ordering for fast foods, she saw this as an opportunity to turn the leftovers into a delicious homemade meal.\nSusan heated the leftover rice and beef in a pan and added soy sauce for flavour. Next, she tossed in the vegetables, creating an aromatic stir fry. Once the meal was ready, Susan served her siblings. To her delight, her siblings loved the meal. This proved that with creativity, leftovers can be turned into a delicious and satisfying meal.\n1. Which values did Susan's actions teach her younger siblings?\n2. From the story, what are the benefits of turning leftovers into a new dish?\n3. Explain other benefits of conserving leftover foods at home.\n4. Share your ideas in class.\nLet us learn\nThe following are the benefits of conserving leftover meals at home:\n1. Prevents wastage of food: Reheating dishes allows one to eat leftover food which ensures that there is no wastage of food.\n2. Saves money: This is because a meal can be produced without buying ingredients.\n3. Saves energy: Reheating dishes does not use a lot of fuel.\n4. Saves labour: Since reheating dishes requires little to no preparation, it saves the effort that would have been used for normal food preparation and cooking.\n5. Saves time: Reheating dishes is faster compared to cooking a meal."},{"page":11,"pdfPage":15,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Preparing leftover foods to avoid wastage"},{"t":"p","html":"We can prepare leftover foods by reheating or recooking. Reheating involves subjecting foods to high temperatures to warm them for eating. Recooking involves creating another dish using leftover foods.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"Manje wants to prepare supper using leftover rice. However, he cannot remember how long the food has stayed in the refrigerator. The rice also has an unusual smell.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What should Manje do? Explain."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"What factors should one consider when using leftover foods?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class. <strong>Caution</strong>"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Throw away leftover foods that show signs of spoilage such as foul smell, unusual colour or unusual texture.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Use digital or print resources to search and compile recipes on how to prepare various leftover foods at home."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"You may also write down recipes used to prepare leftover foods that you use at home."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share the recipes in class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover ugali"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover <em>ugali</em>, knife, margarine, frying pan, source of heat, a clean plate and matchbox.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Slice the <em>ugali</em> into thin slices."}]}],"text":"Preparing leftover foods to avoid wastage\nWe can prepare leftover foods by reheating or recooking. Reheating involves subjecting foods to high temperatures to warm them for eating. Recooking involves creating another dish using leftover foods.\nActivity 4 Let us talk (in pairs)\nManje wants to prepare supper using leftover rice. However, he cannot remember how long the food has stayed in the refrigerator. The rice also has an unusual smell.\n1. What should Manje do? Explain.\n2. What factors should one consider when using leftover foods?\n3. Share your ideas in class. Caution\nThrow away leftover foods that show signs of spoilage such as foul smell, unusual colour or unusual texture.\nActivity 5 Let us go digital\n1. Use digital or print resources to search and compile recipes on how to prepare various leftover foods at home.\n2. You may also write down recipes used to prepare leftover foods that you use at home.\n3. Share the recipes in class.\nActivity 6 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover ugali\nRequirements\nLeftover ugali, knife, margarine, frying pan, source of heat, a clean plate and matchbox.\nSteps to follow\n1. Slice the ugali into thin slices."},{"page":12,"pdfPage":16,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p12-1.jpg","w":353,"h":252,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Leftover ugali being cut into thin slices on a plate."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Spread margarine on both sides of the sliced <em>ugali</em>."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Heat a frying pan."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Place the <em>ugali</em> spread with margarine onto the hot frying pan."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Fry until both sides are browned.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p12-2.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Slices of ugali spread with margarine frying in a pan."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Serve for breakfast on a clean plate. You may serve alongside suitable accompaniments such as tea and an egg.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p12-3.jpg","w":358,"h":251,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Browned ugali toast served on a plate."}]}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 7","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover ugali"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover ugali, 1 egg, salt, tongs or a spoon, cooking oil, frying pan, source of heat, a clean plate and matchbox.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Slice the <em>ugali</em> into thin slices."}]}],"text":"Leftover ugali being cut into thin slices on a plate.\n2. Spread margarine on both sides of the sliced ugali.\n3. Heat a frying pan.\n4. Place the ugali spread with margarine onto the hot frying pan.\n5. Fry until both sides are browned.\nSlices of ugali spread with margarine frying in a pan.\n6. Serve for breakfast on a clean plate. You may serve alongside suitable accompaniments such as tea and an egg.\nBrowned ugali toast served on a plate.\nActivity 7 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover ugali\nRequirements\nLeftover ugali, 1 egg, salt, tongs or a spoon, cooking oil, frying pan, source of heat, a clean plate and matchbox.\nSteps to follow\n1. Slice the ugali into thin slices."},{"page":13,"pdfPage":17,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Beat the egg in a bowl and add some salt to taste."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Coat the slices of <em>ugali</em> with the beaten egg."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Shallow fry both sides of the <em>ugali</em> slices until light brown.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p13-1.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Ugali slices coated with beaten egg, shallow-frying in a pan."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Serve when hot on a clean plate.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p13-2.jpg","w":365,"h":251,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Egg-coated fried ugali slices served on a plate."}]}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 8","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover chapati"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover <em>chapati</em>, source of heat, matchbox, pan, stew or tea.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Dry fry the <em>chapati</em> on a moderately hot pan."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Serve with tea or stew."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 9","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover boiled arrowroots"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover boiled arrowroots, cooking oil, matchbox, source of heat, slotted spoon, clean plate, a deep frying pan, colander or bowl and absorbent paper.","indent":false}],"text":"2. Beat the egg in a bowl and add some salt to taste.\n3. Coat the slices of ugali with the beaten egg.\n4. Shallow fry both sides of the ugali slices until light brown.\nUgali slices coated with beaten egg, shallow-frying in a pan.\n5. Serve when hot on a clean plate.\nEgg-coated fried ugali slices served on a plate.\nActivity 8 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover chapati\nRequirements\nLeftover chapati, source of heat, matchbox, pan, stew or tea.\nSteps to follow\n1. Dry fry the chapati on a moderately hot pan.\n2. Serve with tea or stew.\nActivity 9 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover boiled arrowroots\nRequirements\nLeftover boiled arrowroots, cooking oil, matchbox, source of heat, slotted spoon, clean plate, a deep frying pan, colander or bowl and absorbent paper."},{"page":14,"pdfPage":18,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Put some cooking oil in a frying pan and place it on the source of heat."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Heat the oil until it is hot enough. Using a slotted spoon, lower the leftover boiled arrowroots gently into the hot oil to prevent the oil from splashing."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Fry the arrowroots while gently turning them until they turn golden brown in colour.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p14-1.jpg","w":366,"h":260,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Pieces of boiled arrowroot deep-frying in hot oil, turned with a slotted spoon."}]},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Remove the deep fried arrowroots from the pan using a slotted spoon and place them in a colander or bowl lined with absorbent paper."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Serve the arrowroots on a clean plate. You may serve with a suitable accompaniment such as tea.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p14-2.jpg","w":366,"h":251,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Golden fried arrowroot pieces served on a plate."}]}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 10","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover githeri"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Leftover <em>githeri</em>, pumpkin leaves, cooking oil, potatoes, salt, <em>sufuria</em> with a lid, jug, potato masher or cooking stick, source of heat and matchbox.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Peel potatoes and wash them in clean water."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Put the peeled potatoes in boiling water and place the pumpkin leaves on top of the potatoes so that they can steam as the potatoes boil."}]}],"text":"Steps to follow\n1. Put some cooking oil in a frying pan and place it on the source of heat.\n2. Heat the oil until it is hot enough. Using a slotted spoon, lower the leftover boiled arrowroots gently into the hot oil to prevent the oil from splashing.\n3. Fry the arrowroots while gently turning them until they turn golden brown in colour.\nPieces of boiled arrowroot deep-frying in hot oil, turned with a slotted spoon.\n4. Remove the deep fried arrowroots from the pan using a slotted spoon and place them in a colander or bowl lined with absorbent paper.\n5. Serve the arrowroots on a clean plate. You may serve with a suitable accompaniment such as tea.\nGolden fried arrowroot pieces served on a plate.\nActivity 10 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover githeri\nRequirements\nLeftover githeri, pumpkin leaves, cooking oil, potatoes, salt, sufuria with a lid, jug, potato masher or cooking stick, source of heat and matchbox.\nSteps to follow\n1. Peel potatoes and wash them in clean water.\n2. Put the peeled potatoes in boiling water and place the pumpkin leaves on top of the potatoes so that they can steam as the potatoes boil."},{"page":15,"pdfPage":19,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"When the potatoes are almost cooked, add the <em>githeri</em> and allow it to warm.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p15-1.jpg","w":402,"h":278,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Leftover githeri being added to potatoes cooking in a pot."}]},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Once the <em>githeri</em> is hot, drain the excess water in a jug."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Add salt and mash using the potato masher or cooking stick.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p15-2.jpg","w":376,"h":255,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Githeri and potatoes being mashed in a pot with a potato masher."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"If the <em>mukimo</em> gets too dry, add the drained liquid."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Put the <em>mukimo</em> in a serving dish."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Serve the <em>mukimo</em> on a clean plate when hot.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p15-3.jpg","w":335,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Mukimo served on a plate."}]},{"n":"9.","level":1,"html":"You may serve alongside a suitable accompaniment such as beef or chicken stew."}]}],"text":"3. When the potatoes are almost cooked, add the githeri and allow it to warm.\nLeftover githeri being added to potatoes cooking in a pot.\n4. Once the githeri is hot, drain the excess water in a jug.\n5. Add salt and mash using the potato masher or cooking stick.\nGitheri and potatoes being mashed in a pot with a potato masher.\n6. If the mukimo gets too dry, add the drained liquid.\n7. Put the mukimo in a serving dish.\n8. Serve the mukimo on a clean plate when hot.\nMukimo served on a plate.\n9. You may serve alongside a suitable accompaniment such as beef or chicken stew."},{"page":16,"pdfPage":20,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 11","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Leftover rice"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"2 cups leftover rice, 3 eggs, 1 onion, cooking oil, pan, matchbox and source of heat.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Peel the onion and wash it."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Chop the onion. Fry the onion in the oil until golden brown.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p16-1.jpg","w":363,"h":247,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Chopped onion frying in a pot."}]},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Beat the eggs in a bowl."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Add the leftover rice and beaten egg to the fried onions. Stir continuously using a fork to prevent the mixture from sticking on the pan.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p16-2.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Leftover rice and beaten egg being stirred into the fried onion."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"When ready, serve it on a clean plate.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p16-3.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Egg-fried rice served on a plate."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"You may serve with a suitable accompaniment such as kale, spinach or cabbage."}]}],"text":"Activity 11 Let us do (in groups)\nLeftover rice\nRequirements\n2 cups leftover rice, 3 eggs, 1 onion, cooking oil, pan, matchbox and source of heat.\nSteps to follow\n1. Peel the onion and wash it.\n2. Chop the onion. Fry the onion in the oil until golden brown.\nChopped onion frying in a pot.\n3. Beat the eggs in a bowl.\n4. Add the leftover rice and beaten egg to the fried onions. Stir continuously using a fork to prevent the mixture from sticking on the pan.\nLeftover rice and beaten egg being stirred into the fried onion.\n5. When ready, serve it on a clean plate.\nEgg-fried rice served on a plate.\n6. You may serve with a suitable accompaniment such as kale, spinach or cabbage."},{"page":17,"pdfPage":21,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.2","substrandTitle":"Conserving Leftover Foods","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Note:</strong> Reheat leftover stews as they are. You may add some chopped coriander to improve the flavour.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Take Home Activity"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Prepare leftover foods at home and serve it to your family members."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Share your experiences in class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 12","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"How many times can one reheat leftover food? Explain your reasons.","indent":false},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Never reheat food more than once. This may cause denaturing (destruction) of food nutrients and lead to growth of micro-organisms.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 13","label":"Let us debate"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Organise the classroom for a debate."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Debate on the following topic: <strong>Families should prioritise conserving leftover</strong> <strong>foods to prevent food wastage at home</strong>."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Record points made during the presentation."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Grade 9 learners made posters to educate the community on the importance of conserving leftover foods at home. Which points are they likely to include in their posters?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Which of the following statements are true regarding factors to consider when using leftover foods? Explain reasons for your responses."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Leftover foods should be stored in clean airtight containers."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Leftover foods should be reheated repeatedly."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Spoilt leftover foods should be prepared to avoid wastage."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Reheating leftover food should not be overdone."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"You have the following leftover foods. Explain how you would use them to prepare a meal."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Boiled potatoes."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Fried chicken."}]}],"text":"Note: Reheat leftover stews as they are. You may add some chopped coriander to improve the flavour.\nTake Home Activity\n1. Prepare leftover foods at home and serve it to your family members.\n2. Share your experiences in class.\nActivity 12 Let us talk (in pairs)\nHow many times can one reheat leftover food? Explain your reasons.\nLet us learn\nNever reheat food more than once. This may cause denaturing (destruction) of food nutrients and lead to growth of micro-organisms.\nActivity 13 Let us debate\n1. Organise the classroom for a debate.\n2. Debate on the following topic: Families should prioritise conserving leftover foods to prevent food wastage at home.\n3. Record points made during the presentation.\nAssess yourself\n1. Grade 9 learners made posters to educate the community on the importance of conserving leftover foods at home. Which points are they likely to include in their posters?\n2. Which of the following statements are true regarding factors to consider when using leftover foods? Explain reasons for your responses.\na) Leftover foods should be stored in clean airtight containers.\nb) Leftover foods should be reheated repeatedly.\nc) Spoilt leftover foods should be prepared to avoid wastage.\nd) Reheating leftover food should not be overdone.\n3. You have the following leftover foods. Explain how you would use them to prepare a meal.\na) Boiled potatoes.\nb) Fried chicken."},{"page":18,"pdfPage":22,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.3","substrandTitle":"Integrated Farming","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Integrated Farming","code":"1.3"},{"t":"p","html":"One agricultural enterprise produces waste which can be considered as a resource or input in another agricultural enterprise. For this reason, some households practise a combination of several agricultural enterprises in the same compound in a way that they mutually benefit each other and conserve available resources.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Meaning of integrated farming"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Share your understanding of the term <strong>integrated farming</strong>."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":""},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Discuss some of the possible farming enterprises that may be practised in integrated farming in your locality."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Discuss how the farming enterprises that you have suggested help in the conservation of resources in the environment."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Present to the rest of the class."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Integrated farming is an approach to farming whereby different but compatible agricultural enterprises are practised in a unit of land. For example, dairy farming, poultry farming, rabbit rearing, fish farming, bee keeping, crop farming and tree growing may be practised in a single unit of land.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Components of an integrated farm"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Using a digital device, observe video clips on an integrated farming system. Alternatively, read relevant print materials on components and importance of integrated farming."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Summarise key points on:"},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"Components of an integrated farming system."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"The importance of integrated farming."}]}],"text":"Integrated Farming\nOne agricultural enterprise produces waste which can be considered as a resource or input in another agricultural enterprise. For this reason, some households practise a combination of several agricultural enterprises in the same compound in a way that they mutually benefit each other and conserve available resources.\nMeaning of integrated farming\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. Share your understanding of the term integrated farming.\n2.\na) Discuss some of the possible farming enterprises that may be practised in integrated farming in your locality.\nb) Discuss how the farming enterprises that you have suggested help in the conservation of resources in the environment.\n3. Present to the rest of the class.\nLet us learn\nIntegrated farming is an approach to farming whereby different but compatible agricultural enterprises are practised in a unit of land. For example, dairy farming, poultry farming, rabbit rearing, fish farming, bee keeping, crop farming and tree growing may be practised in a single unit of land.\nComponents of an integrated farm\nActivity 2 Let us go digital\n1. Using a digital device, observe video clips on an integrated farming system. Alternatively, read relevant print materials on components and importance of integrated farming.\n2. Summarise key points on:\na. Components of an integrated farming system.\nb. The importance of integrated farming."},{"page":19,"pdfPage":23,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.3","substrandTitle":"Integrated Farming","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"With the guidance of your teacher, visit a household that practises integrated farming."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Observe how the various farming activities complement each other."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Find out from the members of the household what they considered before establishing an integrated farm."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Write a short report of your findings from the visit."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Present your findings in class."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Examples of an integrated farm"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Study the pictures below which show various farming activities being practised on a 0.4 hectare piece of land.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p19-1.jpg","w":772,"h":571,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"An integrated farm on a small piece of land: a net-covered greenhouse, a raised wooden animal house, maize, tomatoes and rows of cabbages."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Explain why the various farming enterprises constitute integrated farming. <strong>Hint:</strong> Show how they can benefit each other."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Suggest other examples of farming enterprises that can make an integrated farm."}]}],"text":"Activity 3 Let us do (in groups)\n1. With the guidance of your teacher, visit a household that practises integrated farming.\n2. Observe how the various farming activities complement each other.\n3. Find out from the members of the household what they considered before establishing an integrated farm.\n4. Write a short report of your findings from the visit.\n5. Present your findings in class.\nExamples of an integrated farm\nActivity 4 Let us do (in groups)\nStudy the pictures below which show various farming activities being practised on a 0.4 hectare piece of land.\nAn integrated farm on a small piece of land: a net-covered greenhouse, a raised wooden animal house, maize, tomatoes and rows of cabbages.\n1. Explain why the various farming enterprises constitute integrated farming. Hint: Show how they can benefit each other.\n2. Suggest other examples of farming enterprises that can make an integrated farm."},{"page":20,"pdfPage":24,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.3","substrandTitle":"Integrated Farming","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Designing a model of an integrated farm"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Make a model of an integrated farm for a small unit of land or a large farm."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Discuss appropriate farming activities to be included in your integrated farming model. You may choose a combination of enterprises from the following: dairy farming, sheep and goat farming, poultry farming, pig farming, rabbit farming, fish rearing, bee keeping, vegetable farming, fruit farming, cereal production, legume production, fodder production, tree farming, among others."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"On a manila sheet, draw a diagram to illustrate an integrated farming system. Study the example shown and discuss how resources are conserved within the farm.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p20-1.jpg","w":794,"h":205,"caption":"","labels":"Dairy cattle Fish pond Grass pasture, maize and sorghum for silage Fodder legumes and shrubs"}]}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Hints:"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"-","level":0,"html":"Cattle manure can be used to fertilise a fish pond, hence allow growth of various organisms such as planktons which supply food to fish."},{"n":"-","level":0,"html":"Slurry from cattle and waste water from fish pond are used to improve soil fertility leading to high production of grass pasture, maize and sorghum which are then used to feed cattle."},{"n":"-","level":0,"html":"Fodder legumes and shrubs fix nitrogen into the soil. Thus, if planted together with grass, maize and silage, soil fertility is improved leading to more feeds for the cattle."},{"n":"-","level":0,"html":"Fodder legumes and shrubs are sources of proteins for cattle and fish."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"You may also create a farm model at the back of the classroom or any other suitable place in the school compound. Show several farming activities that constitute integrated farming. You may use materials such as clay soil, plasticine, water, offcuts, timber, stones, cartons, cardboards, used polythene sheets, used papers, common vegetation in the area, among others. All the group members should be assigned roles."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Explain the importance of your proposed integrated farming model to other learners in your class."}]}],"text":"Designing a model of an integrated farm\nActivity 5 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Make a model of an integrated farm for a small unit of land or a large farm.\n2. Discuss appropriate farming activities to be included in your integrated farming model. You may choose a combination of enterprises from the following: dairy farming, sheep and goat farming, poultry farming, pig farming, rabbit farming, fish rearing, bee keeping, vegetable farming, fruit farming, cereal production, legume production, fodder production, tree farming, among others.\n3. On a manila sheet, draw a diagram to illustrate an integrated farming system. Study the example shown and discuss how resources are conserved within the farm.\nDairy cattle Fish pond Grass pasture, maize and sorghum for silage Fodder legumes and shrubs\nHints:\n- Cattle manure can be used to fertilise a fish pond, hence allow growth of various organisms such as planktons which supply food to fish.\n- Slurry from cattle and waste water from fish pond are used to improve soil fertility leading to high production of grass pasture, maize and sorghum which are then used to feed cattle.\n- Fodder legumes and shrubs fix nitrogen into the soil. Thus, if planted together with grass, maize and silage, soil fertility is improved leading to more feeds for the cattle.\n- Fodder legumes and shrubs are sources of proteins for cattle and fish.\n4. You may also create a farm model at the back of the classroom or any other suitable place in the school compound. Show several farming activities that constitute integrated farming. You may use materials such as clay soil, plasticine, water, offcuts, timber, stones, cartons, cardboards, used polythene sheets, used papers, common vegetation in the area, among others. All the group members should be assigned roles.\n5. Explain the importance of your proposed integrated farming model to other learners in your class."},{"page":21,"pdfPage":25,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.3","substrandTitle":"Integrated Farming","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of integrated farming"},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Some of the benefits of integrated farming include:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Efficient utilisation of resources and hence reduction of production costs. For example, livestock wastes and fish waste water can be used as manure for crops while crop residues and fodder crops are used as livestock feed."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Soil conservation through use of organic manures and recycling of nutrients between crops, livestock and fish. In addition, when trees are grown on land, their roots hold the soil firmly thus preventing soil erosion by water."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Promotion of environmental sustainability. For example, the use of chemical fertilisers which pollute soil is reduced when organic manures generated in the farm are used in the garden. Animal waste, crop residues and wood within the farm can be used as sources of energy at the household level thus minimising the use of fossil fuels which pollute our environment."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Diversification of income sources throughout the year."},{"n":"e)","level":2,"html":"Promotion of food security and nutrition. Practising different farming activities reduces the risk of total food production failure."},{"n":"f)","level":2,"html":"High yields per unit area."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Before practising integrated farming, it is important to consider various factors such as availability of land, labour, resources and skills to manage the various enterprises; suitability of ecological conditions (rainfall, temperature, soil type and other environmental factors) to the proposed farming enterprises and cost of running the various enterprises.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"When searching for information on components of integrated farming, Grade 9 learners came across an integrated crop-livestock farming system consisting of livestock (cattle, poultry), crops (maize, sorghum and sugarcane) and fodder crops (napier grass, Rhodes grass, lucerne)."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Explain how the above components of the integrated farm conserve available resources."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"What other benefits does such a farming system have over one where only crop farming or livestock farming is practised?"}]}],"text":"Importance of integrated farming\nLet us learn\nSome of the benefits of integrated farming include:\na) Efficient utilisation of resources and hence reduction of production costs. For example, livestock wastes and fish waste water can be used as manure for crops while crop residues and fodder crops are used as livestock feed.\nb) Soil conservation through use of organic manures and recycling of nutrients between crops, livestock and fish. In addition, when trees are grown on land, their roots hold the soil firmly thus preventing soil erosion by water.\nc) Promotion of environmental sustainability. For example, the use of chemical fertilisers which pollute soil is reduced when organic manures generated in the farm are used in the garden. Animal waste, crop residues and wood within the farm can be used as sources of energy at the household level thus minimising the use of fossil fuels which pollute our environment.\nd) Diversification of income sources throughout the year.\ne) Promotion of food security and nutrition. Practising different farming activities reduces the risk of total food production failure.\nf) High yields per unit area.\nBefore practising integrated farming, it is important to consider various factors such as availability of land, labour, resources and skills to manage the various enterprises; suitability of ecological conditions (rainfall, temperature, soil type and other environmental factors) to the proposed farming enterprises and cost of running the various enterprises.\nAssess yourself\n1. When searching for information on components of integrated farming, Grade 9 learners came across an integrated crop-livestock farming system consisting of livestock (cattle, poultry), crops (maize, sorghum and sugarcane) and fodder crops (napier grass, Rhodes grass, lucerne).\na) Explain how the above components of the integrated farm conserve available resources.\nb) What other benefits does such a farming system have over one where only crop farming or livestock farming is practised?"},{"page":22,"pdfPage":26,"strand":"1.0","strandTitle":"Conservation of Resources","substrand":"1.3","substrandTitle":"Integrated Farming","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"During a field visit to a neighbouring farm, Grade 9 learners observed the following:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Huge piles of unused rabbit waste next to the rabbit hutches."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Rabbits depended on fresh grass which was being obtained from the neighbouring farms."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"There was excessive use of pesticides. This resulted in few bees in the orchard and vegetables sections of the garden during the flowering season."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"The different crops in the garden were stunted and unhealthy."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Suggest how the farmer may apply the knowledge and skills of integrated farming to solve the above challenges.","indent":true},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Discuss this statement: \"Integrated farming enhances environmental sustainability.\""}]}],"text":"2. During a field visit to a neighbouring farm, Grade 9 learners observed the following:\na) Huge piles of unused rabbit waste next to the rabbit hutches.\nb) Rabbits depended on fresh grass which was being obtained from the neighbouring farms.\nc) There was excessive use of pesticides. This resulted in few bees in the orchard and vegetables sections of the garden during the flowering season.\nd) The different crops in the garden were stunted and unhealthy.\nSuggest how the farmer may apply the knowledge and skills of integrated farming to solve the above challenges.\n3. Discuss this statement: \"Integrated farming enhances environmental sustainability.\""},{"page":23,"pdfPage":27,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"strand","text":"Food Production Processes","code":"2.0"},{"t":"p","html":"We will learn how to grow crops through organic gardening practices and how to store crop produce in order to prevent post-harvest losses. In addition, we will learn how to prepare food products from various flour mixtures.","indent":false},{"t":"substrand","text":"Organic Gardening","code":"2.1"},{"t":"p","html":"Some of the conventional gardening practices can have negative effects on human health and the environment. In addition, they are costly and thus unsustainable in the long run. Are you aware of such practices? What is the alternative to such practices? One alternative is organic gardening. We will learn how organic gardening can be practised in order to reduce such problems and ensure sustainable food production.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Meaning of organic gardening"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Using a digital device, search the meaning of organic gardening."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Find out the practices or techniques that make organic gardening unique from other types of gardening."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Find out the reasons why organic gardening is being practised."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Share your findings with the class."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Exploring organic gardening practices"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"With the guidance of your teacher, pay a visit to a farm or a nearby agricultural institution where organic gardening is practised."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Identify the crops which are grown in the farm."}]}],"text":"Food Production Processes\nWe will learn how to grow crops through organic gardening practices and how to store crop produce in order to prevent post-harvest losses. In addition, we will learn how to prepare food products from various flour mixtures.\nOrganic Gardening\nSome of the conventional gardening practices can have negative effects on human health and the environment. In addition, they are costly and thus unsustainable in the long run. Are you aware of such practices? What is the alternative to such practices? One alternative is organic gardening. We will learn how organic gardening can be practised in order to reduce such problems and ensure sustainable food production.\nMeaning of organic gardening\nActivity 1 Let us go digital\n1. Using a digital device, search the meaning of organic gardening.\n2. Find out the practices or techniques that make organic gardening unique from other types of gardening.\n3. Find out the reasons why organic gardening is being practised.\n4. Share your findings with the class.\nExploring organic gardening practices\nActivity 2 Let us do (in groups)\n1. With the guidance of your teacher, pay a visit to a farm or a nearby agricultural institution where organic gardening is practised.\n2. Identify the crops which are grown in the farm."},{"page":24,"pdfPage":28,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Find out the following information:"},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"How soil fertility is improved to ensure proper growth of crops."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"Materials or resources used to improve soil fertility."},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"How crop pests and diseases are controlled."},{"n":"d.","level":2,"html":"How weeds are controlled."},{"n":"e.","level":2,"html":"Other gardening practices used to ensure healthy soil as well as safety of crop produce to humans and the environment."},{"n":"f.","level":2,"html":"The benefits of practising organic gardening."}]},{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Note:</strong> You may also seek the information from a resource person.","indent":true},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"In organic gardening, natural inputs such as organic manures, organic foliar feeds and natural pesticides (bio-pesticides) are used instead of synthetic (chemical) fertilisers and pesticides. Cultural practices are used to ensure good environment for crop growth. Examples of cultural practices include selecting pest and disease resistant crops, use of clean seeds, crop rotation, intercropping, cover cropping, mulching, non-chemical weed control, use of trap crops to control pests, proper disposal of diseased crop residues and good sanitation in the gardens. The principle behind organic gardening is to build healthy soil that ensures healthy crops so that they can resist pests and diseases. Organic gardening promotes human health and food safety and also conserves the environment (water, soil and animals including beneficial insects and wildlife).","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Establishing crops using organic techniques"},{"t":"p","html":"How can we grow food crops through organic gardening? We will prepare a planting site, plant a crop and manage it using organic gardening techniques.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify a short season crop (a vegetable, legume or spice) that is best suited to grow in your environment. Some of the vegetables that you may grow are kales, spinach, cabbages, tomatoes, among others. Examples of legumes include common dry beans, garden peas, snow peas, French beans, groundnuts, green grams, cowpeas and pigeon peas. Examples of spices include coriander, chillies, oregano, basil, mint, ginger and rosemary. Consider factors such as availability of planting materials, suitability to the local climate, type of soil as well as pest and disease resistance. You can consult your teacher, parent or guardian and crop production experts in the community."}]}],"text":"3. Find out the following information:\na. How soil fertility is improved to ensure proper growth of crops.\nb. Materials or resources used to improve soil fertility.\nc. How crop pests and diseases are controlled.\nd. How weeds are controlled.\ne. Other gardening practices used to ensure healthy soil as well as safety of crop produce to humans and the environment.\nf. The benefits of practising organic gardening.\nNote: You may also seek the information from a resource person.\nLet us learn\nIn organic gardening, natural inputs such as organic manures, organic foliar feeds and natural pesticides (bio-pesticides) are used instead of synthetic (chemical) fertilisers and pesticides. Cultural practices are used to ensure good environment for crop growth. Examples of cultural practices include selecting pest and disease resistant crops, use of clean seeds, crop rotation, intercropping, cover cropping, mulching, non-chemical weed control, use of trap crops to control pests, proper disposal of diseased crop residues and good sanitation in the gardens. The principle behind organic gardening is to build healthy soil that ensures healthy crops so that they can resist pests and diseases. Organic gardening promotes human health and food safety and also conserves the environment (water, soil and animals including beneficial insects and wildlife).\nEstablishing crops using organic techniques\nHow can we grow food crops through organic gardening? We will prepare a planting site, plant a crop and manage it using organic gardening techniques.\nActivity 3 Let us talk (in groups)\n1. Identify a short season crop (a vegetable, legume or spice) that is best suited to grow in your environment. Some of the vegetables that you may grow are kales, spinach, cabbages, tomatoes, among others. Examples of legumes include common dry beans, garden peas, snow peas, French beans, groundnuts, green grams, cowpeas and pigeon peas. Examples of spices include coriander, chillies, oregano, basil, mint, ginger and rosemary. Consider factors such as availability of planting materials, suitability to the local climate, type of soil as well as pest and disease resistance. You can consult your teacher, parent or guardian and crop production experts in the community."},{"page":25,"pdfPage":29,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Present to the rest of the class, giving reasons why the crop you have chosen should be adopted."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"After presentations, agree on the crop to be grown by the entire class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Download a video clip explaining how to grow the crop that you chose during Activity 3. You may use these search words: \"<strong>video on organic gardening of</strong> <strong>……….\"</strong> (Insert the name of a crop that you intend to grow in the blank spaces). Example: <strong>\"Video on organic gardening of common beans.\"</strong>"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"You may also search for more information from the internet, books and magazines."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Note the cultural practices which are applied when growing the crop."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Preparing a seedbed"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Why should a seedbed be prepared before planting crops?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"What are the characteristics of a good site for preparing a seedbed?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"How do we prepare a seedbed when practising organic gardening?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"The pictures below show various types of seedbeds that may be used for growing crops.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p25-1.jpg","w":757,"h":486,"caption":"Sunken seedbeds; Raised moisture bed","labels":"Flat seed beds Raised seed beds"}]}]}],"text":"2. Present to the rest of the class, giving reasons why the crop you have chosen should be adopted.\n3. After presentations, agree on the crop to be grown by the entire class.\nActivity 4 Let us go digital\n1. Download a video clip explaining how to grow the crop that you chose during Activity 3. You may use these search words: \"video on organic gardening of ……….\" (Insert the name of a crop that you intend to grow in the blank spaces). Example: \"Video on organic gardening of common beans.\"\n2. You may also search for more information from the internet, books and magazines.\n3. Note the cultural practices which are applied when growing the crop.\nPreparing a seedbed\nActivity 5 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. Why should a seedbed be prepared before planting crops?\n2. What are the characteristics of a good site for preparing a seedbed?\n3. How do we prepare a seedbed when practising organic gardening?\n4. The pictures below show various types of seedbeds that may be used for growing crops.\nSunken seedbeds; Raised moisture bed Flat seed beds Raised seed beds"},{"page":26,"pdfPage":30,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p26-1.jpg","w":809,"h":301,"caption":"Container garden; Multi-storey or conical gardens","labels":""},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p26-2.jpg","w":428,"h":306,"caption":"Suspended gardens","labels":""},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p26-3.jpg","w":264,"h":164,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Suspended garden: seedlings growing in cut plastic bottles hung on a wire between two posts."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Discuss the conditions under which each of the above seedbeds is prepared."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Discuss how each of the above seedbeds is prepared."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"In what ways can soil fertility in the seedbed be improved?"},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Share with the rest of the class."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"The best planting site should be selected in order to grow healthy crops. The planting site should be fertile, well drained and free from chemical contamination. It should also receive adequate sunlight. A seedbed is prepared to remove weeds and loosen the soil to ensure proper soil aeration, easy planting and water infiltration. Turning soil helps to destroy various stages of pests by exposing them to the sun or birds. Any infected plant remains should also be removed from the seedbed and destroyed.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"In organic gardening, well rotten organic manures should be incorporated into the soil during seedbed preparation. The organic manures should always be ready during the time of seedbed preparation and planting. In the absence of compost or farm yard manure, green manure can also be used to improve soil fertility. Leguminous plants are planted on a seedbed and when they reach flowering stage, they are ploughed into the soil so that they can decompose to release nutrients. An example of a green manuring plant is Mexican sunflower <em>(tithonia)</em>.","indent":false}],"text":"Container garden; Multi-storey or conical gardens\nSuspended gardens\nSuspended garden: seedlings growing in cut plastic bottles hung on a wire between two posts.\na) Discuss the conditions under which each of the above seedbeds is prepared.\nb) Discuss how each of the above seedbeds is prepared.\nc) In what ways can soil fertility in the seedbed be improved?\n5. Share with the rest of the class.\nLet us learn\nThe best planting site should be selected in order to grow healthy crops. The planting site should be fertile, well drained and free from chemical contamination. It should also receive adequate sunlight. A seedbed is prepared to remove weeds and loosen the soil to ensure proper soil aeration, easy planting and water infiltration. Turning soil helps to destroy various stages of pests by exposing them to the sun or birds. Any infected plant remains should also be removed from the seedbed and destroyed.\nIn organic gardening, well rotten organic manures should be incorporated into the soil during seedbed preparation. The organic manures should always be ready during the time of seedbed preparation and planting. In the absence of compost or farm yard manure, green manure can also be used to improve soil fertility. Leguminous plants are planted on a seedbed and when they reach flowering stage, they are ploughed into the soil so that they can decompose to release nutrients. An example of a green manuring plant is Mexican sunflower (tithonia)."},{"page":27,"pdfPage":31,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p27-1.jpg","w":725,"h":621,"caption":"Mexican sunflower (tithonia)","labels":""},{"t":"p","html":"In case drainage is poor, the seedbed can be raised to prevent crop diseases especially those that affect roots. In places where drainage is good and rainfall is adequate, flat seedbeds are suitable. However, in dry areas, water retention seedbeds such as sunken seedbeds and shallow pits (Zai pits) can be prepared. In case there is shortage of space, innovative container gardening such as multi-storey gardens and suspended gardens may be used.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify a suitable planting site in your school compound."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Agree on the type of seedbed or seedbeds for establishing your preferred crop or crops."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Write down the tools and equipment as well as other materials that you will require for preparing your seedbed."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Use the tools, equipment and other materials that you have identified to prepare a suitable seedbed for raising the crop or crops chosen. In case seedlings require to be raised in a nursery bed, prepare the nursery bed first, establish the seedlings and take care until they are ready for transplanting to the seedbed."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Ensure you mix the soil with well decomposed compost manure. You may also use farm yard manure from cattle, sheep, goats, pigs, rabbits or poultry.","indent":false}],"text":"Mexican sunflower (tithonia)\nIn case drainage is poor, the seedbed can be raised to prevent crop diseases especially those that affect roots. In places where drainage is good and rainfall is adequate, flat seedbeds are suitable. However, in dry areas, water retention seedbeds such as sunken seedbeds and shallow pits (Zai pits) can be prepared. In case there is shortage of space, innovative container gardening such as multi-storey gardens and suspended gardens may be used.\nActivity 6 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Identify a suitable planting site in your school compound.\n2. Agree on the type of seedbed or seedbeds for establishing your preferred crop or crops.\n3. Write down the tools and equipment as well as other materials that you will require for preparing your seedbed.\n4. Use the tools, equipment and other materials that you have identified to prepare a suitable seedbed for raising the crop or crops chosen. In case seedlings require to be raised in a nursery bed, prepare the nursery bed first, establish the seedlings and take care until they are ready for transplanting to the seedbed.\nEnsure you mix the soil with well decomposed compost manure. You may also use farm yard manure from cattle, sheep, goats, pigs, rabbits or poultry."},{"page":28,"pdfPage":32,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Note"},{"t":"p","html":"Observe safety when using the tools to avoid injuries.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Clean your tools and equipment after preparing your seedbed.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Planting a crop"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 7","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Look for suitable planting materials such as seeds, seedlings, tubers, and cuttings for the selected crop and bring them to the class. Your teacher may also assist in providing the planting materials."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Ensure the planting materials selected are of high quality. How can you tell that the seeds or seedlings are of high quality?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Plant the selected crop on a moist seedbed. Ask your teacher the number of seeds to plant per hole, the depth of planting holes and the spacing of the holes between the rows and within the rows.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p28-1.jpg","w":500,"h":326,"caption":"Sowing seeds in a sunken seedbed","labels":""},{"src":"media/book-figures/p28-2.jpg","w":512,"h":374,"caption":"Sowing seeds in container seedbeds","labels":""}]}]}],"text":"Note\nObserve safety when using the tools to avoid injuries.\nClean your tools and equipment after preparing your seedbed.\nPlanting a crop\nActivity 7 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Look for suitable planting materials such as seeds, seedlings, tubers, and cuttings for the selected crop and bring them to the class. Your teacher may also assist in providing the planting materials.\n2. Ensure the planting materials selected are of high quality. How can you tell that the seeds or seedlings are of high quality?\n3. Plant the selected crop on a moist seedbed. Ask your teacher the number of seeds to plant per hole, the depth of planting holes and the spacing of the holes between the rows and within the rows.\nSowing seeds in a sunken seedbed\nSowing seeds in container seedbeds"},{"page":29,"pdfPage":33,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Seeds for planting should be wholesome, clean, dry and pest and disease free. The seeds or other planting materials should be obtained from healthy and disease resistant crops so that they tolerate the effects of pests and diseases. The selected planting materials should also be suited to the climate and soil of a given place for better survival and high yields. For example, sorghum, cassava, green grams, cowpeas and pigeon peas grow well in semi-arid areas because they are drought resistant. This is unlike green peas, potatoes and cabbages which do well in cool and moist areas. Growing crops that are not well adapted to the environment will lead to higher chances of attack by pests and diseases. This leads to produce of low quality and quantity. Proper planting depth will enable seeds to germinate with ease. On the other hand, proper spacing ensures that the crops receive adequate nutrients, water, sunlight and air for proper growth.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p29-1.jpg","w":787,"h":576,"caption":"How to space pigeon peas","labels":""},{"t":"h3","text":"Care for the growing crop"},{"t":"p","html":"Once the planting materials are planted, they require care for them to grow well. Maintaining crop nutrients and managing pests and diseases are key in organic farming. We will therefore learn how to prepare organic fertilisers and pesticides. We shall then apply the knowledge and skills learnt to take care of our crops. We will also carry out other cultural practices on the growing crops.","indent":false}],"text":"Let us learn\nSeeds for planting should be wholesome, clean, dry and pest and disease free. The seeds or other planting materials should be obtained from healthy and disease resistant crops so that they tolerate the effects of pests and diseases. The selected planting materials should also be suited to the climate and soil of a given place for better survival and high yields. For example, sorghum, cassava, green grams, cowpeas and pigeon peas grow well in semi-arid areas because they are drought resistant. This is unlike green peas, potatoes and cabbages which do well in cool and moist areas. Growing crops that are not well adapted to the environment will lead to higher chances of attack by pests and diseases. This leads to produce of low quality and quantity. Proper planting depth will enable seeds to germinate with ease. On the other hand, proper spacing ensures that the crops receive adequate nutrients, water, sunlight and air for proper growth.\nHow to space pigeon peas\nCare for the growing crop\nOnce the planting materials are planted, they require care for them to grow well. Maintaining crop nutrients and managing pests and diseases are key in organic farming. We will therefore learn how to prepare organic fertilisers and pesticides. We shall then apply the knowledge and skills learnt to take care of our crops. We will also carry out other cultural practices on the growing crops."},{"page":30,"pdfPage":34,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 8","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What cultural practices should be carried out on your growing crop?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"At what stage of crop growth is each of the cultural practices done?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Why is each of the cultural practices important?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"How are organic foliar feeds or fertilisers prepared?"},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"How are organic pesticides prepared?"},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Apart from using organic pesticides, how else are pests and diseases controlled organically?"},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Share with the rest of the class."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Making foliar feed or fertiliser"},{"t":"p","html":"It is important to top dress the growing crops with organic foliar feed. Top dressing is done when the crop has developed adequate roots and leaves so as to absorb the nutrients in the foliar feed or fertiliser. We will learn how to prepare foliar feeds using organic manures or soft plant materials.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 9","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Put some compost in an empty 20 litre bucket. The compost should fill about a third of the bucket."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Add clean fresh water to fill the bucket and stir thoroughly with a stake to make a solution as shown below.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p30-1.jpg","w":323,"h":376,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A learner stirring compost and water in a bucket with a stick to make liquid manure."}]},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Put a lid on the bucket and place it in a well sheltered place for a few days."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Pour the solution through a piece of cloth into another bucket to remove the solid matter."}]}],"text":"Activity 8 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. What cultural practices should be carried out on your growing crop?\n2. At what stage of crop growth is each of the cultural practices done?\n3. Why is each of the cultural practices important?\n4. How are organic foliar feeds or fertilisers prepared?\n5. How are organic pesticides prepared?\n6. Apart from using organic pesticides, how else are pests and diseases controlled organically?\n7. Share with the rest of the class.\nMaking foliar feed or fertiliser\nIt is important to top dress the growing crops with organic foliar feed. Top dressing is done when the crop has developed adequate roots and leaves so as to absorb the nutrients in the foliar feed or fertiliser. We will learn how to prepare foliar feeds using organic manures or soft plant materials.\nActivity 9 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Put some compost in an empty 20 litre bucket. The compost should fill about a third of the bucket.\n2. Add clean fresh water to fill the bucket and stir thoroughly with a stake to make a solution as shown below.\nA learner stirring compost and water in a bucket with a stick to make liquid manure.\n3. Put a lid on the bucket and place it in a well sheltered place for a few days.\n4. Pour the solution through a piece of cloth into another bucket to remove the solid matter."},{"page":31,"pdfPage":35,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p31-1.jpg","w":308,"h":403,"caption":"Filtering compost-water mixture","labels":""},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Add fresh clean water to the liquid manure collected in the bucket to dilute it. The volume of water should be twice that of the liquid manure. Your liquid manure, also known as compost tea is now ready for application on your growing crop. <strong>Note</strong>","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p31-2.jpg","w":390,"h":415,"caption":"Applying liquid manure to a crop","labels":""}]}]},{"t":"p","html":"You may use farm yard manure from chicken, pigs, cattle, sheep, goats and rabbits to make liquid manure if compost manure is not available.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"An alternative method of making liquid manure is to suspend a sack of manure inside a drum containing clean water for 7 days. Nutrients will be released slowly into the water. The liquid manure should be diluted with water before applying to the crops. Rabbit urine has also been found to be a good foliar feed (bio fertiliser). However, it should be diluted with water in a ratio of 1:2 to avoid scorching the crops.","indent":false}],"text":"Filtering compost-water mixture\n5. Add fresh clean water to the liquid manure collected in the bucket to dilute it. The volume of water should be twice that of the liquid manure. Your liquid manure, also known as compost tea is now ready for application on your growing crop. Note\nApplying liquid manure to a crop\nYou may use farm yard manure from chicken, pigs, cattle, sheep, goats and rabbits to make liquid manure if compost manure is not available.\nAn alternative method of making liquid manure is to suspend a sack of manure inside a drum containing clean water for 7 days. Nutrients will be released slowly into the water. The liquid manure should be diluted with water before applying to the crops. Rabbit urine has also been found to be a good foliar feed (bio fertiliser). However, it should be diluted with water in a ratio of 1:2 to avoid scorching the crops."},{"page":32,"pdfPage":36,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 10","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Obtain soft sappy material (leaves and stems) from a leguminous plant such as Mexican sunflower."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Chop it into small pieces."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Add the material into a bucket or drum with clean water. The ratio between the material and the water may be 1:4."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Cover the bucket and leave for about 7 days to allow nutrients to be released into the water as the plant material breaks down."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Dilute the foliar feed with water at a ratio of 1:10."}]},{"t":"p","html":"What plant materials can you use to make foliar feed or fertiliser? Enquire from your teacher and also search from the internet.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Making natural pesticides"},{"t":"p","html":"Natural pesticides are environmentally friendly when compared to synthetic pesticides. The aim is to repel pests so as to keep them away from the crops. We will prepare some natural pesticides (bio pesticides) using locally available materials to control pests and some diseases in our growing crops. The best time to spray is early in the morning or the evening. This helps to avoid disintegration from the sun and to minimise harm to predators and beneficial insects such as bees. A few methods of preparing natural pesticides are provided below.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using ash brew"},{"t":"p","html":"The alkaline nature of ash may deter some pests that attack crops. Ash from burnt organic materials such as wood or plant residues is mixed thoroughly with clean water in a ratio of 1:10 in a clean container such as a bucket or drum. This means that for every cup of ash, 10 cups of water are used. The mixture is left to soak for a few days or a week depending on the type of material used to make the ash (soft or hard). Bar soap is grated into small pieces and then added into the mixture. The ratio of the soap to ash should be 1:10. The new mixture is then boiled while stirring for about 30 minutes to enhance melting of the soap. The mixture should then be allowed to cool for 1 to 2 hours. After the ash has settled, the liquid (ash brew) should be poured into another container leaving the settled ash behind. Any remaining ash particles can be filtered out using a fine mesh strainer or cheese cloth. The natural pesticide can be sprayed on the leaves, stems and surrounding soil around the crop where pests have been noticed. It may be reapplied after rain or when its effectiveness decreases.","indent":false}],"text":"Activity 10 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Obtain soft sappy material (leaves and stems) from a leguminous plant such as Mexican sunflower.\n2. Chop it into small pieces.\n3. Add the material into a bucket or drum with clean water. The ratio between the material and the water may be 1:4.\n4. Cover the bucket and leave for about 7 days to allow nutrients to be released into the water as the plant material breaks down.\n5. Dilute the foliar feed with water at a ratio of 1:10.\nWhat plant materials can you use to make foliar feed or fertiliser? Enquire from your teacher and also search from the internet.\nMaking natural pesticides\nNatural pesticides are environmentally friendly when compared to synthetic pesticides. The aim is to repel pests so as to keep them away from the crops. We will prepare some natural pesticides (bio pesticides) using locally available materials to control pests and some diseases in our growing crops. The best time to spray is early in the morning or the evening. This helps to avoid disintegration from the sun and to minimise harm to predators and beneficial insects such as bees. A few methods of preparing natural pesticides are provided below.\nUsing ash brew\nThe alkaline nature of ash may deter some pests that attack crops. Ash from burnt organic materials such as wood or plant residues is mixed thoroughly with clean water in a ratio of 1:10 in a clean container such as a bucket or drum. This means that for every cup of ash, 10 cups of water are used. The mixture is left to soak for a few days or a week depending on the type of material used to make the ash (soft or hard). Bar soap is grated into small pieces and then added into the mixture. The ratio of the soap to ash should be 1:10. The new mixture is then boiled while stirring for about 30 minutes to enhance melting of the soap. The mixture should then be allowed to cool for 1 to 2 hours. After the ash has settled, the liquid (ash brew) should be poured into another container leaving the settled ash behind. Any remaining ash particles can be filtered out using a fine mesh strainer or cheese cloth. The natural pesticide can be sprayed on the leaves, stems and surrounding soil around the crop where pests have been noticed. It may be reapplied after rain or when its effectiveness decreases."},{"page":33,"pdfPage":37,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Precautions"},{"t":"p","html":"Ash containing harmful chemicals should not be used. For example, ash from plastic materials and wood which has been treated and painted.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Ash should not be allowed into the spray bottle or knapsack sprayer as it can clog the nozzle.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"One should wear protective gear such as gloves and safety goggles when handling the ash or the ash brew.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Before spraying all the crops, one should spray a small area to ensure that the crops can tolerate the ash brew.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using hot pepper"},{"t":"p","html":"500 grams of hot pepper fruits are crushed and then mixed with 5 litres of water. The mixture is boiled for 20 minutes and allowed to cool. After sieving the mixture, 5 litres of water mixed with bar soap is added to the filtrate. The pesticide can be used to control ants, aphids, flies and caterpillars.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using garlic"},{"t":"p","html":"10 to 12 cloves of crushed garlic are mixed with 1 litre of water. After settling for 24 hours, the mixture is strained using a piece of cloth. A cup of cooking oil is then added to the filtrate and then sprayed on the crop. A mixture of garlic, onion and water can also be used as a pesticide. The strong smell of the spray repels the pests away from the crops.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using pyrethrum spray"},{"t":"p","html":"20 g of pyrethrum powder is mixed with 10 litres of water. Soap may be added to enhance long lasting effects. The mixture is then strained and applied immediately on the pests. In addition, dry pyrethrum flowers may be ground into powder and then dusted over insect pests.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using baking soda spray"},{"t":"p","html":"Four teaspoons of baking soda are mixed in one litre of water. The mixture is added to two tablespoons of vegetable oil and two tablespoons of liquid soap and shaken vigorously.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Milk spray"},{"t":"p","html":"One litre of milk is mixed with one litre of water and then sprayed to control mildew, a fungal disease in some vegetable crops.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Using Sodom apple and Mexican marigold"},{"t":"p","html":"Fruits of Sodom apple and Mexican marigold leaves are crushed and then soaked in water for 2 weeks. Some liquid soap may be added to assist the mixture to stick on the crops. The pesticide is used to repel aphids and other pests.","indent":false}],"text":"Precautions\nAsh containing harmful chemicals should not be used. For example, ash from plastic materials and wood which has been treated and painted.\nAsh should not be allowed into the spray bottle or knapsack sprayer as it can clog the nozzle.\nOne should wear protective gear such as gloves and safety goggles when handling the ash or the ash brew.\nBefore spraying all the crops, one should spray a small area to ensure that the crops can tolerate the ash brew.\nUsing hot pepper\n500 grams of hot pepper fruits are crushed and then mixed with 5 litres of water. The mixture is boiled for 20 minutes and allowed to cool. After sieving the mixture, 5 litres of water mixed with bar soap is added to the filtrate. The pesticide can be used to control ants, aphids, flies and caterpillars.\nUsing garlic\n10 to 12 cloves of crushed garlic are mixed with 1 litre of water. After settling for 24 hours, the mixture is strained using a piece of cloth. A cup of cooking oil is then added to the filtrate and then sprayed on the crop. A mixture of garlic, onion and water can also be used as a pesticide. The strong smell of the spray repels the pests away from the crops.\nUsing pyrethrum spray\n20 g of pyrethrum powder is mixed with 10 litres of water. Soap may be added to enhance long lasting effects. The mixture is then strained and applied immediately on the pests. In addition, dry pyrethrum flowers may be ground into powder and then dusted over insect pests.\nUsing baking soda spray\nFour teaspoons of baking soda are mixed in one litre of water. The mixture is added to two tablespoons of vegetable oil and two tablespoons of liquid soap and shaken vigorously.\nMilk spray\nOne litre of milk is mixed with one litre of water and then sprayed to control mildew, a fungal disease in some vegetable crops.\nUsing Sodom apple and Mexican marigold\nFruits of Sodom apple and Mexican marigold leaves are crushed and then soaked in water for 2 weeks. Some liquid soap may be added to assist the mixture to stick on the crops. The pesticide is used to repel aphids and other pests."},{"page":34,"pdfPage":38,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Note"},{"t":"p","html":"It is good to try what works best since there is no recommended amount of pesticide to apply unlike when using synthetic chemicals. Other natural pesticides include rabbit urine, neem oil, tobacco spray, tomato and black nightshade spray and wood ash. You may find out how such homemade pesticides are used to control crop pests in the garden. In addition, there are safe bio-pesticides being sold in the market.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Other ways of managing pests and diseases in crops"},{"t":"h5","text":"Field hygiene or sanitation"},{"t":"p","html":"Seriously infected plants should be uprooted and destroyed to prevent spreading pests and diseases. This practice is called <em>roguing.</em> In addition, clean tools and equipment should be used in the garden to avoid introducing diseases in the garden.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Flooding and irrigation"},{"t":"p","html":"Flooding minimises pests which hide in the soil, for example, cutworms. Overhead irrigation can help to dislodge aphids from the leaves and drown spider mites. However, the two methods should target the specific pests since they can bring more problems. For example, flooding soil can affect respiration of crop roots and also lead to soil erosion. Overhead irrigation, on the other hand, can increase chances of diseases that affect the crop leaves.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Natural predators"},{"t":"p","html":"It is important to encourage natural predators of pests in the garden. For example, chameleons, ladybird beetles, spiders, green lacewings, parasitic wasps and praying mantis eat various insect pests that attack crops. It is important to have a variety of plants that provide habitat for such beneficial organisms in an organic garden.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Crop rotation"},{"t":"p","html":"Crops which are related should be planted in a rotation programme because they are attacked by similar pests and diseases. For example, tomatoes should not be planted in a site where potatoes, eggplant and chillies had been planted. Similarly, watermelon should not be planted where cucumber, pumpkin or butternut had been planted in the previous season.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Companion planting"},{"t":"p","html":"This refers to close planting of different plants to enhance each other's growth and protect them from pests. For example, vegetables can be planted together with some herbs such as basil, mint and rosemary which serve as pest repellents. Carrots and onions are also good companion crops for repelling onion flies and carrot flies respectively.","indent":false}],"text":"Note\nIt is good to try what works best since there is no recommended amount of pesticide to apply unlike when using synthetic chemicals. Other natural pesticides include rabbit urine, neem oil, tobacco spray, tomato and black nightshade spray and wood ash. You may find out how such homemade pesticides are used to control crop pests in the garden. In addition, there are safe bio-pesticides being sold in the market.\nOther ways of managing pests and diseases in crops\nField hygiene or sanitation\nSeriously infected plants should be uprooted and destroyed to prevent spreading pests and diseases. This practice is called roguing. In addition, clean tools and equipment should be used in the garden to avoid introducing diseases in the garden.\nFlooding and irrigation\nFlooding minimises pests which hide in the soil, for example, cutworms. Overhead irrigation can help to dislodge aphids from the leaves and drown spider mites. However, the two methods should target the specific pests since they can bring more problems. For example, flooding soil can affect respiration of crop roots and also lead to soil erosion. Overhead irrigation, on the other hand, can increase chances of diseases that affect the crop leaves.\nNatural predators\nIt is important to encourage natural predators of pests in the garden. For example, chameleons, ladybird beetles, spiders, green lacewings, parasitic wasps and praying mantis eat various insect pests that attack crops. It is important to have a variety of plants that provide habitat for such beneficial organisms in an organic garden.\nCrop rotation\nCrops which are related should be planted in a rotation programme because they are attacked by similar pests and diseases. For example, tomatoes should not be planted in a site where potatoes, eggplant and chillies had been planted. Similarly, watermelon should not be planted where cucumber, pumpkin or butternut had been planted in the previous season.\nCompanion planting\nThis refers to close planting of different plants to enhance each other's growth and protect them from pests. For example, vegetables can be planted together with some herbs such as basil, mint and rosemary which serve as pest repellents. Carrots and onions are also good companion crops for repelling onion flies and carrot flies respectively."},{"page":35,"pdfPage":39,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Weed control"},{"t":"p","html":"Some weeds harbour a number of pests and disease-causing organisms (fungi, bacteria and viruses) which can easily spread to the crops. Therefore, non-chemical methods of weed control such as mulching, uprooting weeds and tilling land are recommended to suppress weed growth. It is important to avoid tilling land when soil is too wet or when it is raining because diseases can spread fast under such conditions.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 11","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Monitor your growing crop regularly throughout the growing season. Carry out appropriate cultural practices on your growing crops. Inform your teacher immediately you notice any unusual appearance on your crop. In case pests are few, hand pick them and destroy them. Your teacher will guide you more on how to deal with pests and diseases in case they appear on your growing crop."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Share responsibilities among the members of the group using a duty schedule like the one shown below."}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Sample project duty schedule"},{"t":"table","rows":[[["Group A Name of crop: French beans Name of group leader: Peter Musyoka"],[""],[""],[""]],[["Date"],["Cultural practice"],["Responsibility allocated to"],["Remarks by the teacher"]],[[""],["Gapping"],["Sheilla, Hassan, Danson, Mary"],["Well done."]],[[""],["Thinning"],["Abdi, Otieno, Hilda, Christine"],["Not yet completed. Members were reminded to thin excess seedlings."]],[[""],["Mulching"],[""],[""]],[[""],["Irrigation (watering)"],[""],[""]],[[""],["Mechanical weeding"],[""],[""]],[[""],["Top dressing (using organic foliar feeds or manures)"],[""],[""]],[[""],["Pest and disease control by cultural methods (specify)"],[""],[""]],[[""],["Others (specify)"],[""],[""]]]}],"text":"Weed control\nSome weeds harbour a number of pests and disease-causing organisms (fungi, bacteria and viruses) which can easily spread to the crops. Therefore, non-chemical methods of weed control such as mulching, uprooting weeds and tilling land are recommended to suppress weed growth. It is important to avoid tilling land when soil is too wet or when it is raining because diseases can spread fast under such conditions.\nActivity 11 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Monitor your growing crop regularly throughout the growing season. Carry out appropriate cultural practices on your growing crops. Inform your teacher immediately you notice any unusual appearance on your crop. In case pests are few, hand pick them and destroy them. Your teacher will guide you more on how to deal with pests and diseases in case they appear on your growing crop.\n2. Share responsibilities among the members of the group using a duty schedule like the one shown below.\nSample project duty schedule\nGroup A Name of crop: French beans Name of group leader: Peter Musyoka |  |  | \nDate | Cultural practice | Responsibility allocated to | Remarks by the teacher\n | Gapping | Sheilla, Hassan, Danson, Mary | Well done.\n | Thinning | Abdi, Otieno, Hilda, Christine | Not yet completed. Members were reminded to thin excess seedlings.\n | Mulching |  | \n | Irrigation (watering) |  | \n | Mechanical weeding |  | \n | Top dressing (using organic foliar feeds or manures) |  | \n | Pest and disease control by cultural methods (specify) |  | \n | Others (specify) |  | "},{"page":36,"pdfPage":40,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Note"},{"t":"p","html":"You may take photographs as you carry out the various gardening practices on your crop. Save the photographs in your portfolio for assessment by your teacher. Ensure the photographs have dates to show when the gardening practices were done. You may also keep a journal to record your gardening activities as well as any successes and challenges experienced.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Harvesting of crops"},{"t":"p","html":"Organically grown crops, just like other crops should be harvested at the appropriate stage of maturity in order to get maximum benefit and minimise field losses.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 12","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"p","html":"We will learn how to harvest crops.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Use digital devices to find out the following information:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"The right stage of harvesting vegetables, legumes and spices."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"The part of the vegetable, legume and spice that is harvested."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"How the various vegetables, legumes and spices are harvested."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"The precautions that should be observed when harvesting the crops."}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Note"},{"t":"p","html":"In each category pick any two crops that you are familiar with. Ensure you include the crop that you have grown in your garden. You may observe videos on how such crops are harvested. Where digital devices are not available contact a resource person to explain how harvesting is done.","indent":false}],"text":"Note\nYou may take photographs as you carry out the various gardening practices on your crop. Save the photographs in your portfolio for assessment by your teacher. Ensure the photographs have dates to show when the gardening practices were done. You may also keep a journal to record your gardening activities as well as any successes and challenges experienced.\nHarvesting of crops\nOrganically grown crops, just like other crops should be harvested at the appropriate stage of maturity in order to get maximum benefit and minimise field losses.\nActivity 12 Let us go digital\nWe will learn how to harvest crops.\n1. Use digital devices to find out the following information:\na) The right stage of harvesting vegetables, legumes and spices.\nb) The part of the vegetable, legume and spice that is harvested.\nc) How the various vegetables, legumes and spices are harvested.\nd) The precautions that should be observed when harvesting the crops.\nNote\nIn each category pick any two crops that you are familiar with. Ensure you include the crop that you have grown in your garden. You may observe videos on how such crops are harvested. Where digital devices are not available contact a resource person to explain how harvesting is done."},{"page":37,"pdfPage":41,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Summarise your findings in a table like the one shown below."}]},{"t":"table","rows":[[["Name of crop"],["Part that is harvested"],["Right stage of harvesting (signs indicating harvest readiness)"],["How the crop is harvested"],["Precautions to observe while harvesting"]],[["Spinach"],["Leaves"],["When spinach has large, fresh, tender and dark green mature leaves. This will be 2 to 3 months after planting."],["Using a thumb and forefinger, pinch off the large outer leaves close to the base of the plant, but just above the top of the soil level. You can also use a knife or pair of scissors to cut off the stems."],["Allow the inner leaves to grow.","After harvesting, remove old yellow or brown leaves from the bunch and discard them.","Harvesting can be done one or two times in a week until the crop stops growing."]],[["Kale"],["Leaves"],["After about 6 weeks from the time of transplanting. Check for kale that has firm, deeply coloured leaves and moist hardy stems."],["Using hands, knife or scissors, pluck the lower large leaves first and then work your way up the plant. Harvesting can be done for 4 to 6 months depending on the care given to the crop."],["3 to 4 top leaves should not be harvested.","A small part of the stalk (about 2.5 cm) should be left attached to avoid damaging the stem.","Avoid picking the terminal bud which is the top centre of the plant to maintain the crop's productivity."]],[["Dry beans"],["Pods"],["When most of the pods are dry and turn brown. The seeds inside the pod should be hard."],["By pulling the whole plant by hand. The dry pods are threshed. This is followed by winnowing, then sorting in order to have clean seeds."],["Harvesting should be done in the morning and when weather is dry."]]]}],"text":"2. Summarise your findings in a table like the one shown below.\nName of crop | Part that is harvested | Right stage of harvesting (signs indicating harvest readiness) | How the crop is harvested | Precautions to observe while harvesting\nSpinach | Leaves | When spinach has large, fresh, tender and dark green mature leaves. This will be 2 to 3 months after planting. | Using a thumb and forefinger, pinch off the large outer leaves close to the base of the plant, but just above the top of the soil level. You can also use a knife or pair of scissors to cut off the stems. | Allow the inner leaves to grow. After harvesting, remove old yellow or brown leaves from the bunch and discard them. Harvesting can be done one or two times in a week until the crop stops growing.\nKale | Leaves | After about 6 weeks from the time of transplanting. Check for kale that has firm, deeply coloured leaves and moist hardy stems. | Using hands, knife or scissors, pluck the lower large leaves first and then work your way up the plant. Harvesting can be done for 4 to 6 months depending on the care given to the crop. | 3 to 4 top leaves should not be harvested. A small part of the stalk (about 2.5 cm) should be left attached to avoid damaging the stem. Avoid picking the terminal bud which is the top centre of the plant to maintain the crop's productivity.\nDry beans | Pods | When most of the pods are dry and turn brown. The seeds inside the pod should be hard. | By pulling the whole plant by hand. The dry pods are threshed. This is followed by winnowing, then sorting in order to have clean seeds. | Harvesting should be done in the morning and when weather is dry."},{"page":38,"pdfPage":42,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"table","rows":[[["Groundnuts"],["Pods"],["When seeds in the pods are big enough to fill the pods. When the pods are pressed by fingers, they split easily."],["Whole plant is lifted from the ground to obtain the pods because they are underground. They are allowed to dry in the sun for a few days, then shelled to remove the seeds. Winnowing and sorting are then done to obtain clean seeds."],["When the weather is dry to prevent rotting of seeds."]],[["Coriander (Dhania)"],["Leaves"],["After about 4 to 6 weeks after planting."],["Whole plants with leaves are uprooted or cut at the base."],["When leaves are big and still green not feathery or yellow.","Large plants should be harvested first to allow the growth of the small plants."]]]},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p38-1.jpg","w":761,"h":344,"caption":"Harvesting of kale; Harvesting of carrots","labels":""},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p38-2.jpg","w":360,"h":297,"caption":"Harvesting of sweet pepper","labels":""},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p38-3.jpg","w":342,"h":296,"caption":"Uprooting mature dry beans","labels":""}],"text":"Groundnuts | Pods | When seeds in the pods are big enough to fill the pods. When the pods are pressed by fingers, they split easily. | Whole plant is lifted from the ground to obtain the pods because they are underground. They are allowed to dry in the sun for a few days, then shelled to remove the seeds. Winnowing and sorting are then done to obtain clean seeds. | When the weather is dry to prevent rotting of seeds.\nCoriander (Dhania) | Leaves | After about 4 to 6 weeks after planting. | Whole plants with leaves are uprooted or cut at the base. | When leaves are big and still green not feathery or yellow. Large plants should be harvested first to allow the growth of the small plants.\nHarvesting of kale; Harvesting of carrots\nHarvesting of sweet pepper\nUprooting mature dry beans"},{"page":39,"pdfPage":43,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p39-1.jpg","w":436,"h":361,"caption":"Uprooting groundnuts by hand","labels":""},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 13","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Using the knowledge and skills acquired, harvest your crop once it is ready. Observe hygiene and ensure you handle your harvested crop especially the vegetables and spices with care to avoid causing injuries and bruises."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Your teacher will guide you on what to do with the harvested crop produce."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of organic gardening"},{"t":"p","html":"We will create educative messages on the benefits of organic gardening.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 14","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Prepare creative messages that you will use to sensitise your community on the health benefits of eating organically grown foods.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Display such messages in appropriate places in the school. See a sample poster below.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p39-2.jpg","w":455,"h":371,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Sample poster with a basket of fresh fruit and vegetables and the words: Organic Gardening, for good human health and a safe and healthy environment."}],"text":"Uprooting groundnuts by hand\nActivity 13 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Using the knowledge and skills acquired, harvest your crop once it is ready. Observe hygiene and ensure you handle your harvested crop especially the vegetables and spices with care to avoid causing injuries and bruises.\n2. Your teacher will guide you on what to do with the harvested crop produce.\nImportance of organic gardening\nWe will create educative messages on the benefits of organic gardening.\nActivity 14 Let us do (in groups)\nPrepare creative messages that you will use to sensitise your community on the health benefits of eating organically grown foods.\nDisplay such messages in appropriate places in the school. See a sample poster below.\nSample poster with a basket of fresh fruit and vegetables and the words: Organic Gardening, for good human health and a safe and healthy environment."},{"page":40,"pdfPage":44,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Some of the benefits of organic gardening include:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"We avoid harmful chemicals that can harm the growers, consumers and animals including beneficial insects in the environment. The organic pesticides used have short residue effect and decay easily when compared to synthetic pesticides."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"It is cheaper since we save on costs of buying the synthetic chemicals."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Helps in environmental conservation, for example when natural wastes are converted into manure."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Helps to improve soil quality. The organic manure supplies nutrients not only to the crops but also to useful soil organisms such as earthworms, some fungi and some bacteria which help in decomposing organic wastes. In addition, organic manure enables the soil to hold enough moisture for crop growth."},{"n":"e)","level":2,"html":"Farmers can access export markets in countries where demand for organic products is high as long as they adhere to the required standards."}]},{"t":"p","html":"However, organic farming requires high labour and more time compared to conventional gardening.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which of the following would an organic gardener <strong>not</strong> use when growing crops? A) Compost manure. B) Poultry droppings. C) Artificial fertiliser. D) Organic mulch."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"In a text box next to each sentence, place a <strong>\"T\"</strong> if the statement is <strong>TRUE</strong> and an <strong>\"F\"</strong> if the statement is <strong>FALSE</strong> as demonstrated in the first statement below."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Soil and water conservation measures are important in organic gardening."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Most crops grow well on waterlogged soils."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Mixing organic manure with soil makes the soil more permeable to water."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Organically grown crops are safe to eat because they do not have chemical residues."},{"n":"e)","level":2,"html":"Using clean tools while working in a garden can prevent the spread of diseases on crops."},{"n":"f)","level":2,"html":"Mouldy, wet and broken seeds can be selected for sowing."},{"n":"g)","level":2,"html":"Legume crops such as beans and peas that are intended for storage as dry grains are harvested when the pods are small and have green colour."}]}],"text":"Let us learn\nSome of the benefits of organic gardening include:\na) We avoid harmful chemicals that can harm the growers, consumers and animals including beneficial insects in the environment. The organic pesticides used have short residue effect and decay easily when compared to synthetic pesticides.\nb) It is cheaper since we save on costs of buying the synthetic chemicals.\nc) Helps in environmental conservation, for example when natural wastes are converted into manure.\nd) Helps to improve soil quality. The organic manure supplies nutrients not only to the crops but also to useful soil organisms such as earthworms, some fungi and some bacteria which help in decomposing organic wastes. In addition, organic manure enables the soil to hold enough moisture for crop growth.\ne) Farmers can access export markets in countries where demand for organic products is high as long as they adhere to the required standards.\nHowever, organic farming requires high labour and more time compared to conventional gardening.\nAssess yourself\n1. Which of the following would an organic gardener not use when growing crops? A) Compost manure. B) Poultry droppings. C) Artificial fertiliser. D) Organic mulch.\n2. In a text box next to each sentence, place a \"T\" if the statement is TRUE and an \"F\" if the statement is FALSE as demonstrated in the first statement below.\na) Soil and water conservation measures are important in organic gardening.\nb) Most crops grow well on waterlogged soils.\nc) Mixing organic manure with soil makes the soil more permeable to water.\nd) Organically grown crops are safe to eat because they do not have chemical residues.\ne) Using clean tools while working in a garden can prevent the spread of diseases on crops.\nf) Mouldy, wet and broken seeds can be selected for sowing.\ng) Legume crops such as beans and peas that are intended for storage as dry grains are harvested when the pods are small and have green colour."},{"page":41,"pdfPage":45,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.1","substrandTitle":"Organic Gardening","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"h)","level":2,"html":"Sunken beds can be used for raising some crops in dry areas."},{"n":"i)","level":2,"html":"Regular monitoring of the growing crops is necessary to check presence of weeds, pests and diseases."},{"n":"j)","level":2,"html":"Thinning increases the number of crop plants in a garden."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Choose the best option in the brackets."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Organically grown legumes are safe and healthy to eat. <strong>[true or false].</strong>"},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Use of agricultural chemicals is discouraged because if proper precautions are not observed, beneficial organisms are killed. <strong>[true or false].</strong>"},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"If domestic animals eat remains of crops that have residues of agricultural chemicals, nothing will happen to our health. <strong>[true or false].</strong>"},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"When people consume food which contains residues of chemicals that were sprayed on the crop, they can <strong>[become sick; become healed].</strong>"},{"n":"e)","level":2,"html":"Several incidences of pest attack are an indication of <strong>[good</strong> <strong>health of the soil and crops</strong>, <strong>poor health of the soil and crop]</strong>."},{"n":"f)","level":2,"html":"Organically grown crops do not attract insect pests because they have <strong>[soft and succulent stems, hard stems]</strong>"},{"n":"g)","level":2,"html":"Overhead irrigation in some crops such as tomatoes <strong>[increases spread</strong> <strong>of diseases</strong>, <strong>reduces spread of diseases]</strong>"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"A Grade 9 learner heard some farmers complaining that artificial fertilisers and pesticides are becoming increasingly expensive."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"What can the farmers use in the absence of artificial fertilisers?"},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Apart from cost, give two other disadvantages of using artificial fertilisers in a garden."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Describe how a farmer can prepare a natural pesticide to reduce the cost of production."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Farmers in your community are interested in organic gardening. Give any six organic gardening practices that they may carry out on their gardens."}]}],"text":"h) Sunken beds can be used for raising some crops in dry areas.\ni) Regular monitoring of the growing crops is necessary to check presence of weeds, pests and diseases.\nj) Thinning increases the number of crop plants in a garden.\n3. Choose the best option in the brackets.\na) Organically grown legumes are safe and healthy to eat. [true or false].\nb) Use of agricultural chemicals is discouraged because if proper precautions are not observed, beneficial organisms are killed. [true or false].\nc) If domestic animals eat remains of crops that have residues of agricultural chemicals, nothing will happen to our health. [true or false].\nd) When people consume food which contains residues of chemicals that were sprayed on the crop, they can [become sick; become healed].\ne) Several incidences of pest attack are an indication of [good health of the soil and crops, poor health of the soil and crop].\nf) Organically grown crops do not attract insect pests because they have [soft and succulent stems, hard stems]\ng) Overhead irrigation in some crops such as tomatoes [increases spread of diseases, reduces spread of diseases]\n4. A Grade 9 learner heard some farmers complaining that artificial fertilisers and pesticides are becoming increasingly expensive.\na) What can the farmers use in the absence of artificial fertilisers?\nb) Apart from cost, give two other disadvantages of using artificial fertilisers in a garden.\nc) Describe how a farmer can prepare a natural pesticide to reduce the cost of production.\n5. Farmers in your community are interested in organic gardening. Give any six organic gardening practices that they may carry out on their gardens."},{"page":42,"pdfPage":46,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.2","substrandTitle":"Storage of Crop Produce","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Storage of Crop Produce","code":"2.2"},{"t":"p","html":"Crop produce gets spoilt if it is not stored appropriately. Poor storage of crop produce can also lead to contamination of the produce making it unhealthy for human consumption. Therefore, it is important to prepare the storage facilities to ensure we have healthy foods. Which facilities are used to store crop produce in our school and homes? How can we prepare the facilities where we store foods in our schools and homes?","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Crop storage structures"},{"t":"p","html":"Crop produce include grains, cereals and tubers that are either bought or harvested from our gardens. Before storing the crop produce, we should prepare the facilities where we store the crop produce. Juma's family grows maize. They harvest the maize and store it in a granary. Hamisi's guardians grow groundnuts. They harvest them, dry them and store them in a big jar.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p42-1.jpg","w":430,"h":330,"caption":"Traditional granary","labels":""},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p42-2.jpg","w":395,"h":317,"caption":"Drum or plastic jar","labels":""},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which structures do you use to store crop produce at home or school?"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Other facilities that can be used to store crop produce include modern granaries, store rooms, storage bags and small containers such as plastic or glass jars and earthen pots.","indent":false}],"text":"Storage of Crop Produce\nCrop produce gets spoilt if it is not stored appropriately. Poor storage of crop produce can also lead to contamination of the produce making it unhealthy for human consumption. Therefore, it is important to prepare the storage facilities to ensure we have healthy foods. Which facilities are used to store crop produce in our school and homes? How can we prepare the facilities where we store foods in our schools and homes?\nCrop storage structures\nCrop produce include grains, cereals and tubers that are either bought or harvested from our gardens. Before storing the crop produce, we should prepare the facilities where we store the crop produce. Juma's family grows maize. They harvest the maize and store it in a granary. Hamisi's guardians grow groundnuts. They harvest them, dry them and store them in a big jar.\nTraditional granary\nDrum or plastic jar\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. Which structures do you use to store crop produce at home or school?\nOther facilities that can be used to store crop produce include modern granaries, store rooms, storage bags and small containers such as plastic or glass jars and earthen pots."},{"page":43,"pdfPage":47,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.2","substrandTitle":"Storage of Crop Produce","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Ways of preparing storage structures for crop produce"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"How can you prepare the storage of crop produce at home or school?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"How can we prepare the storage structures shown in the following pictures before storing crop produce?","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p43-1.jpg","w":506,"h":280,"caption":"Modern granary","labels":""},{"src":"media/book-figures/p43-2.jpg","w":440,"h":341,"caption":"Pest proof storage bags","labels":""},{"src":"media/book-figures/p43-3.jpg","w":458,"h":329,"caption":"Storage containers","labels":""}]}]}],"text":"Ways of preparing storage structures for crop produce\nActivity 2 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. How can you prepare the storage of crop produce at home or school?\n2. How can we prepare the storage structures shown in the following pictures before storing crop produce?\nModern granary\nPest proof storage bags\nStorage containers"},{"page":44,"pdfPage":48,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.2","substrandTitle":"Storage of Crop Produce","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"Before storing the crop produce in the storage structures and facilities, they should be prepared to ensure that they are safe to handle crop produce. Storage facilities and structures should keep the produce free from water to avoid rotting, free from dust to avoid contamination and secure from pests such as rats and weevils.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Juma's family prepares the granary in the following ways before storing maize:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"They clean the granary to remove wastes from the previous crop remains."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"They seal all the openings where rainwater can pass through."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"They clean and dry the storage bags."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"They dust the granary with some pesticide to kill any weevils and pests in the granary."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Hamisi's guardian prepares the grain storage container by:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Cleaning the container to remove any remains from previous crop produce."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Drying the container to ensure it has no wet parts."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Cleaning the lid and ensuring it is tightly fitting to prevent entry of pests."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Preparation of a storage facility or structure for storing crop produce"},{"t":"p","html":"Preparation of a structure or facility for storing crop produce should be done to ensure that the produce is stored in a safe place away from contamination. It also maintains the quality of the stored crop produce.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Preparing a storage facility or structure for storing crop produce"},{"t":"p","html":"Use the ideas you have gained from your previous discussion to prepare a storage structure or facility in readiness for storing crop produce. At home, seek guidance of your parents or guardians. While at school, you will be guided by your teacher to prepare the store where cereals and pulses are stored. Prepare the storage structure or facility by carrying out the following:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Removing all the remains of the previous crop produce."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Sweeping or cleaning the storage room."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Removing any form of dust, dirt, remains of previous content of the storage containers, bad smell and unpleasant odours."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Opening the room or structure to allow fresh air circulation."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Sealing parts such as the roof of the granary to prevent leaking."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Replacing any broken parts such as the rodent guards for the granary."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Dusting the store room or granary to kill any pests."}]}],"text":"Before storing the crop produce in the storage structures and facilities, they should be prepared to ensure that they are safe to handle crop produce. Storage facilities and structures should keep the produce free from water to avoid rotting, free from dust to avoid contamination and secure from pests such as rats and weevils.\nJuma's family prepares the granary in the following ways before storing maize:\n1. They clean the granary to remove wastes from the previous crop remains.\n2. They seal all the openings where rainwater can pass through.\n3. They clean and dry the storage bags.\n4. They dust the granary with some pesticide to kill any weevils and pests in the granary.\nHamisi's guardian prepares the grain storage container by:\n1. Cleaning the container to remove any remains from previous crop produce.\n2. Drying the container to ensure it has no wet parts.\n3. Cleaning the lid and ensuring it is tightly fitting to prevent entry of pests.\nPreparation of a storage facility or structure for storing crop produce\nPreparation of a structure or facility for storing crop produce should be done to ensure that the produce is stored in a safe place away from contamination. It also maintains the quality of the stored crop produce.\nActivity 3 Preparing a storage facility or structure for storing crop produce\nUse the ideas you have gained from your previous discussion to prepare a storage structure or facility in readiness for storing crop produce. At home, seek guidance of your parents or guardians. While at school, you will be guided by your teacher to prepare the store where cereals and pulses are stored. Prepare the storage structure or facility by carrying out the following:\n1. Removing all the remains of the previous crop produce.\n2. Sweeping or cleaning the storage room.\n3. Removing any form of dust, dirt, remains of previous content of the storage containers, bad smell and unpleasant odours.\n4. Opening the room or structure to allow fresh air circulation.\n5. Sealing parts such as the roof of the granary to prevent leaking.\n6. Replacing any broken parts such as the rodent guards for the granary.\n7. Dusting the store room or granary to kill any pests."},{"page":45,"pdfPage":49,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.2","substrandTitle":"Storage of Crop Produce","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Managing stored crop produce to reduce spoilage"},{"t":"p","html":"Stored crop produce should be handled appropriately while in the storage facilities and structures to ensure that they remain in good condition. Poor handling of the produce can lead to rotting and contamination of the produce.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"How can you care for the crop produce in your storage facilities at home?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"How can you care for the crop produce in the storage room at school?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"How can we be responsible in managing our stored crop produce? Your teacher will guide you on how to check the storage room in school and carry out any necessary practices to prevent spoilage of the stored crop produce. You can practise the learnt skills at home."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"The following are some practices that are carried out in a store or storage facility to reduce spoilage of crop produce:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Check the storage room and containers to ensure they are always dry with no signs of wetness and exposure to cold weather."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Ensure the storage room is always cool and dry."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Use a dry container to fetch the grains from the storage sacks."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Check to ensure that there is adequate air circulation in the storage room. This controls dampness and accumulation of bad odours."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Check to remove any contamination or spoilt produce that may lead to growth of moulds in the storage facility."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Check and remove any crop produce that has signs of pests, diseases or spoilage to prevent spread."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Avoid returning partially cut crop produce such as fruits, cabbages and pumpkins to the storage facility."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Clear vegetation around the store room or granary."},{"n":"9.","level":1,"html":"Maintain the storage facility in clean condition."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Proper storage of crop produce reduces food spoilage to ensure that we have food at all times. Therefore, it enhances food security.","indent":false}],"text":"Managing stored crop produce to reduce spoilage\nStored crop produce should be handled appropriately while in the storage facilities and structures to ensure that they remain in good condition. Poor handling of the produce can lead to rotting and contamination of the produce.\nActivity 4 Let us do (in groups)\n1. How can you care for the crop produce in your storage facilities at home?\n2. How can you care for the crop produce in the storage room at school?\n3. How can we be responsible in managing our stored crop produce? Your teacher will guide you on how to check the storage room in school and carry out any necessary practices to prevent spoilage of the stored crop produce. You can practise the learnt skills at home.\nLet us learn\nThe following are some practices that are carried out in a store or storage facility to reduce spoilage of crop produce:\n1. Check the storage room and containers to ensure they are always dry with no signs of wetness and exposure to cold weather.\n2. Ensure the storage room is always cool and dry.\n3. Use a dry container to fetch the grains from the storage sacks.\n4. Check to ensure that there is adequate air circulation in the storage room. This controls dampness and accumulation of bad odours.\n5. Check to remove any contamination or spoilt produce that may lead to growth of moulds in the storage facility.\n6. Check and remove any crop produce that has signs of pests, diseases or spoilage to prevent spread.\n7. Avoid returning partially cut crop produce such as fruits, cabbages and pumpkins to the storage facility.\n8. Clear vegetation around the store room or granary.\n9. Maintain the storage facility in clean condition.\nProper storage of crop produce reduces food spoilage to ensure that we have food at all times. Therefore, it enhances food security."},{"page":46,"pdfPage":50,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.2","substrandTitle":"Storage of Crop Produce","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Why should the storage facility be kept dry?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"List three causes of wetness in a storage facility."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Explain how you can reduce bad odours in a granary."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Jamila bought a plastic drum for storing her grains. Explain how she can prepare the drum before storing the grains."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Hamisi found some rotten grains in the sack while fetching maize for cooking. What should he do to ensure food safety of the stored maize?"},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Jane wanted to keep their granary clean. Her mother told her to sweep the granary. Why did her mother advise her to sweep the granary and not mop it with water?"},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"List the caring practices you would carry out to ensure grains stored in your school store are safe."}]}],"text":"Assess yourself\n1. Why should the storage facility be kept dry?\n2. List three causes of wetness in a storage facility.\n3. Explain how you can reduce bad odours in a granary.\n4. Jamila bought a plastic drum for storing her grains. Explain how she can prepare the drum before storing the grains.\n5. Hamisi found some rotten grains in the sack while fetching maize for cooking. What should he do to ensure food safety of the stored maize?\n6. Jane wanted to keep their granary clean. Her mother told her to sweep the granary. Why did her mother advise her to sweep the granary and not mop it with water?\n7. List the caring practices you would carry out to ensure grains stored in your school store are safe."},{"page":47,"pdfPage":51,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","code":"2.3"},{"t":"p","html":"Flour mixtures are a combination of wheat flour, raising agents and other ingredients such as fat, salt, sugar and liquid (milk, eggs, orange juice or water).","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Types of flour mixtures"},{"t":"p","html":"Flour can be mixed in different ways to get a range of products. The types of flour mixtures also depend on the proportion of flour and other ingredients used.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify the types of flour mixtures shown in the pictures below.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p47-1.jpg","w":803,"h":338,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Picture a: a thin, runny flour mixture flowing from a spoon into a bowl. Picture b: a smooth ball of soft flour mixture."}]},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Use print or digital resources to find the definitions of the following terms in relation to flour mixtures:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Dough."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Batter."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Present your findings in class."}]},{"t":"p","html":"The following are the types of flour mixtures:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Batter: This flour mixture has a flowing consistency. There are two types of batter: thick batter and thin batter."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Dough: This flour mixture is soft but firm. It is also elastic and can be kneaded or rolled."}]}],"text":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures\nFlour mixtures are a combination of wheat flour, raising agents and other ingredients such as fat, salt, sugar and liquid (milk, eggs, orange juice or water).\nTypes of flour mixtures\nFlour can be mixed in different ways to get a range of products. The types of flour mixtures also depend on the proportion of flour and other ingredients used.\nActivity 1 Let us go digital\n1. Identify the types of flour mixtures shown in the pictures below.\nPicture a: a thin, runny flour mixture flowing from a spoon into a bowl. Picture b: a smooth ball of soft flour mixture.\n2. Use print or digital resources to find the definitions of the following terms in relation to flour mixtures:\na) Dough.\nb) Batter.\n3. Present your findings in class.\nThe following are the types of flour mixtures:\na) Batter: This flour mixture has a flowing consistency. There are two types of batter: thick batter and thin batter.\nb) Dough: This flour mixture is soft but firm. It is also elastic and can be kneaded or rolled."},{"page":48,"pdfPage":52,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Preparing flour mixtures"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, prepare batter.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"2 cups of flour, 1 egg, 2 cups of water, salt, hand whisk or spoon, cup and a basin or bowl.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Sieve the flour and salt in a bowl or basin and make a hole in the centre of the flour.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p48-1.jpg","w":435,"h":308,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Flour being sieved into a bowl."}]},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Break the egg in a cup and beat it lightly."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Pour in the beaten egg with a little water. Beat the mixture until the batter becomes smooth. Add water as necessary to create the desired consistency of batter.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p48-2.jpg","w":413,"h":298,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Batter being mixed with a spoon in a bowl."}]},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Cover and leave it to stand."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Use the batter as required."}]}],"text":"Preparing flour mixtures\nActivity 2 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, prepare batter.\nRequirements\n2 cups of flour, 1 egg, 2 cups of water, salt, hand whisk or spoon, cup and a basin or bowl.\nWhat to do\n1. Sieve the flour and salt in a bowl or basin and make a hole in the centre of the flour.\nFlour being sieved into a bowl.\n2. Break the egg in a cup and beat it lightly.\n3. Pour in the beaten egg with a little water. Beat the mixture until the batter becomes smooth. Add water as necessary to create the desired consistency of batter.\nBatter being mixed with a spoon in a bowl.\n4. Cover and leave it to stand.\n5. Use the batter as required."},{"page":49,"pdfPage":53,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, prepare dough.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"250 grams flour, 100 grams of margarine or cooking oil or fat, 150 millilitres of milk or water, sugar and salt.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Sift flour, sugar and salt together."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Add margarine or fat to the flour and rub it in."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Add the milk or water and knead to produce dough."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Put the dough aside, cover it with a dry cloth and leave it to stand for thirty minutes.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p49-1.jpg","w":272,"h":206,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A ball of dough resting in a bowl under a cloth, with a rolling pin."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Knead the dough again."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Roll out the dough and shape as desired."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Making products from various flour mixtures"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"How can we make products from flour mixtures?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify the products shown in the following pictures.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p49-2.jpg","w":736,"h":258,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Picture a: a stack of chapatis. Picture b: potato pieces coated in batter and fried (viazi karai) with sauce."}]}]}],"text":"Activity 3 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, prepare dough.\nRequirements\n250 grams flour, 100 grams of margarine or cooking oil or fat, 150 millilitres of milk or water, sugar and salt.\nWhat to do\n1. Sift flour, sugar and salt together.\n2. Add margarine or fat to the flour and rub it in.\n3. Add the milk or water and knead to produce dough.\n4. Put the dough aside, cover it with a dry cloth and leave it to stand for thirty minutes.\nA ball of dough resting in a bowl under a cloth, with a rolling pin.\n5. Knead the dough again.\n6. Roll out the dough and shape as desired.\nMaking products from various flour mixtures\nActivity 4 Let us talk (in pairs)\nHow can we make products from flour mixtures?\nActivity 5 Let us talk (in pairs)\n1. Identify the products shown in the following pictures.\nPicture a: a stack of chapatis. Picture b: potato pieces coated in batter and fried (viazi karai) with sauce."},{"page":50,"pdfPage":54,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p50-1.jpg","w":688,"h":290,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Picture c: a bowl of mandazi. Picture d: a stack of pancakes."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Which flour mixtures have been used to make the products shown in pictures (a) to (d)?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"List other products made using batter and dough."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"We can make products from flour mixtures by shallow frying, baking and deep frying."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Dough is used to make <em>chapati</em>, <em>mandazi</em>, <em>mahamri</em>, doughnuts and bread."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Thin batter is used to make pancakes."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Thick batter is used for coating foods such as fish, chicken and potatoes (<em>viazi</em> <em>karai</em>). It can also be used to make drop scones."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, prepare pancakes.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Use the recipe for making batter provided in (<em><strong>Activity 2, page 48</strong></em>)."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Heat a little amount of cooking oil in the frying pan."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Pour the batter to cover the base of the pan thinly and cook until browned.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p50-2.jpg","w":366,"h":252,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Batter being poured into a hot pan to make a pancake."}]}]}],"text":"Picture c: a bowl of mandazi. Picture d: a stack of pancakes.\n2. Which flour mixtures have been used to make the products shown in pictures (a) to (d)?\n3. List other products made using batter and dough.\n4. Share your ideas in class.\nLet us learn\n1. We can make products from flour mixtures by shallow frying, baking and deep frying.\n2. Dough is used to make chapati, mandazi, mahamri, doughnuts and bread.\n3. Thin batter is used to make pancakes.\n4. Thick batter is used for coating foods such as fish, chicken and potatoes (viazi karai). It can also be used to make drop scones.\nActivity 6 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, prepare pancakes.\nWhat to do\n1. Use the recipe for making batter provided in (Activity 2, page 48).\n2. Heat a little amount of cooking oil in the frying pan.\n3. Pour the batter to cover the base of the pan thinly and cook until browned.\nBatter being poured into a hot pan to make a pancake."},{"page":51,"pdfPage":55,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Flip the pancake over when bubbles start to form on the surface.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p51-1.jpg","w":365,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A pancake cooking in a pan."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Cook the other side until browned."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Place the pancake on the plate."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Cook all pancakes. Serve hot on a clean plate.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p51-2.jpg","w":363,"h":251,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A cooked pancake on a plate."}]}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 7","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, prepare <em>chapati</em>.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"250 grams flour, basin or bowl, knife, cooking oil, warm water, salt, shallow frying pan, basting brush or spoon, fish slice, rolling pin and rolling board.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Use the recipe for making dough provided in (<em><strong>Activity 3, page 49)</strong></em>. Roll the dough on a lightly floured rolling board and lightly brush the rolled out dough with some oil.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p51-3.jpg","w":365,"h":232,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Chapati dough being rolled out with a rolling pin."}]}]}],"text":"4. Flip the pancake over when bubbles start to form on the surface.\nA pancake cooking in a pan.\n5. Cook the other side until browned.\n6. Place the pancake on the plate.\n7. Cook all pancakes. Serve hot on a clean plate.\nA cooked pancake on a plate.\nActivity 7 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, prepare chapati.\nRequirements\n250 grams flour, basin or bowl, knife, cooking oil, warm water, salt, shallow frying pan, basting brush or spoon, fish slice, rolling pin and rolling board.\nWhat to do\n1. Use the recipe for making dough provided in (Activity 3, page 49). Roll the dough on a lightly floured rolling board and lightly brush the rolled out dough with some oil.\nChapati dough being rolled out with a rolling pin."},{"page":52,"pdfPage":56,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Cut the rolled <em>chapati</em> dough into strips and roll them into a spiral shape to the desired size. Repeat this process with the rest of the strips.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p52-1.jpg","w":738,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Rolled chapati dough cut into strips, and the strips coiled into round balls."}]},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Lightly flour the rolling board and roll out each of the balls of coiled dough to form a circular shape."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Preheat a pan and place the <em>chapati</em> dough to cook for about one minute.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p52-2.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A chapati cooking in a pan."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Flip the <em>chapati</em> and allow the other side to cook for one minute. Brush the top surface with little oil.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p52-3.jpg","w":366,"h":248,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A chapati being brushed with a little oil while it cooks."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Flip the <em>chapati</em> over and let it cook until you achieve your desired brownness. Smear the top dry part with a little oil then flip to cook to the desired brownness."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Repeat steps 3 to 6 with the rest of the coiled shaped dough balls."}]}],"text":"2. Cut the rolled chapati dough into strips and roll them into a spiral shape to the desired size. Repeat this process with the rest of the strips.\nRolled chapati dough cut into strips, and the strips coiled into round balls.\n3. Lightly flour the rolling board and roll out each of the balls of coiled dough to form a circular shape.\n4. Preheat a pan and place the chapati dough to cook for about one minute.\nA chapati cooking in a pan.\n5. Flip the chapati and allow the other side to cook for one minute. Brush the top surface with little oil.\nA chapati being brushed with a little oil while it cooks.\n6. Flip the chapati over and let it cook until you achieve your desired brownness. Smear the top dry part with a little oil then flip to cook to the desired brownness.\n7. Repeat steps 3 to 6 with the rest of the coiled shaped dough balls."},{"page":53,"pdfPage":57,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 8","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, prepare <em>mandazi</em>.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"250 grams self raising flour, margarine, basin or bowl, an egg, cooking oil, warm water or milk, knife, sugar, deep frying pan, slotted spoon, rolling pin and rolling board.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Use the recipe for making dough provided in <strong>(</strong><em><strong>Activity 3, page 49)</strong></em>."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Roll the dough on a lightly floured rolling board."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Using a knife, cut out the rolled dough into the desired shapes.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p53-1.jpg","w":398,"h":268,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Rolled dough cut into squares for mandazi."}]},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Add cooking oil in the deep frying pan and place it on the source of heat. The pan should not be more than two thirds full for safety."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"When the oil is hot enough, use a slotted spoon to gently lower the shaped pieces of dough into the hot oil to prevent the oil from splashing.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p53-2.jpg","w":389,"h":270,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Mandazi deep-frying in hot oil, lifted with a slotted spoon."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Fry the dough in hot oil until it browns."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Flip the <em>mandazi</em> and fry the other side until brown."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Serve the <em>mandazi</em> on a clean plate."}]}],"text":"Activity 8 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, prepare mandazi.\nRequirements\n250 grams self raising flour, margarine, basin or bowl, an egg, cooking oil, warm water or milk, knife, sugar, deep frying pan, slotted spoon, rolling pin and rolling board.\nWhat to do\n1. Use the recipe for making dough provided in (Activity 3, page 49).\n2. Roll the dough on a lightly floured rolling board.\n3. Using a knife, cut out the rolled dough into the desired shapes.\nRolled dough cut into squares for mandazi.\n4. Add cooking oil in the deep frying pan and place it on the source of heat. The pan should not be more than two thirds full for safety.\n5. When the oil is hot enough, use a slotted spoon to gently lower the shaped pieces of dough into the hot oil to prevent the oil from splashing.\nMandazi deep-frying in hot oil, lifted with a slotted spoon.\n6. Fry the dough in hot oil until it browns.\n7. Flip the mandazi and fry the other side until brown.\n8. Serve the mandazi on a clean plate."},{"page":54,"pdfPage":58,"strand":"2.0","strandTitle":"Food Production Processes","substrand":"2.3","substrandTitle":"Cooking: Using Flour Mixtures","blocks":[{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p54-1.jpg","w":366,"h":264,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Fried mandazi served on a plate."},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which of the following statements are true regarding batter and dough?"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Batter has a high ratio of water to flour."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"There are two types of dough; thin dough and thick dough."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Food items made using dough include pancakes."},{"n":"d)","level":2,"html":"Thick batter is used for coating foods."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Which food items can be prepared using:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Batter."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Dough."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Solve the following riddles:"},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"I am thin and can be poured with ease in a pan. Pancakes or drop scones, I am part of the plan. Who am I?"},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"I am soft and stretchy. Knead me well, let me rise. I can also be moulded and shaped. Who am I?"}]}],"text":"Fried mandazi served on a plate.\nAssess yourself\n1. Which of the following statements are true regarding batter and dough?\na) Batter has a high ratio of water to flour.\nb) There are two types of dough; thin dough and thick dough.\nc) Food items made using dough include pancakes.\nd) Thick batter is used for coating foods.\n2. Which food items can be prepared using:\na) Batter.\nb) Dough.\n3. Solve the following riddles:\na) I am thin and can be poured with ease in a pan. Pancakes or drop scones, I am part of the plan. Who am I?\nb) I am soft and stretchy. Knead me well, let me rise. I can also be moulded and shaped. Who am I?"},{"page":55,"pdfPage":59,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"strand","text":"Hygiene Practices","code":"3.0"},{"t":"p","html":"Hygiene refers to cleanliness. Hygiene practices involve various practices that promote health, cleanliness and well-being in our daily lives. From personal hygiene to household sanitation practices, maintaining cleanliness is essential in creating a safe environment for us.","indent":false},{"t":"substrand","text":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","code":"3.1"},{"t":"p","html":"Waste disposal facilities refer to systems designed for the collection and disposal of various types of waste generated by households. These facilities play a crucial role in proper management of waste.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"How do you dispose of waste at home?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify the waste disposal facilities shown in the following pictures.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p55-1.jpg","w":699,"h":371,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Picture a: a bathroom sink. Picture b: a waste bin with a push lid. Picture c: an open drain beside a road."}]}]}],"text":"Hygiene Practices\nHygiene refers to cleanliness. Hygiene practices involve various practices that promote health, cleanliness and well-being in our daily lives. From personal hygiene to household sanitation practices, maintaining cleanliness is essential in creating a safe environment for us.\nCleaning Waste Disposal Facilities\nWaste disposal facilities refer to systems designed for the collection and disposal of various types of waste generated by households. These facilities play a crucial role in proper management of waste.\nImportance of cleaning waste disposal facilities\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in pairs)\nHow do you dispose of waste at home?\nActivity 2 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Identify the waste disposal facilities shown in the following pictures.\nPicture a: a bathroom sink. Picture b: a waste bin with a push lid. Picture c: an open drain beside a road."},{"page":56,"pdfPage":60,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Take a walk around your school."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Identify the waste disposal facilities used in school."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Brainstorm on the type of waste <em>(liquid or solid)</em> each disposal facility you identified handles."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us do (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"David, a Grade 9 learner, researched on the Internet for information on the various waste disposal facilities. He formulated riddles using his findings. Read and solve each riddle.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p56-1.jpg","w":737,"h":445,"caption":"","labels":"1. I am a fixture commonly found in both kitchens and bathrooms. In the kitchen, I am utilised to clean utensils. In the bathroom, I serve the purpose of facilitating hand washing and other personal hygiene activities. What am I? 2. I am a narrow ditch used to drain waste water from buildings to main gutters. I am open at the surface, you can see as waste water flows. What am I? 3. I am a container with a crucial role. Preventing littering and keeping the environment clean. In me, waste finds a temporary stay. Until proper disposal, I heed the call. What am I?"},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Waste bin: It is also known as a trash bin, garbage can or dustbin. It is a container specifically designed for the temporary storage and disposal of solid waste. Waste bins hold waste until it can be properly disposed of."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Sink: This is a plumbing fixture that is fixed to the wall in a kitchen or bathroom. It consists of a basin with a tap for supplying water and a drain for removing waste water."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Open drains: They are narrow channels or ditches designed to drain waste water or rain water away from buildings to main gutters by the roadsides. The main gutters then carry waste water out of the town. Open drains range from well-constructed ones made from cement, metals or plastic to simple trenches dug on the ground."}]}],"text":"2. Take a walk around your school.\na) Identify the waste disposal facilities used in school.\nb) Brainstorm on the type of waste (liquid or solid) each disposal facility you identified handles.\n3. Share your ideas in class.\nActivity 3 Let us do (in pairs)\nDavid, a Grade 9 learner, researched on the Internet for information on the various waste disposal facilities. He formulated riddles using his findings. Read and solve each riddle.\n1. I am a fixture commonly found in both kitchens and bathrooms. In the kitchen, I am utilised to clean utensils. In the bathroom, I serve the purpose of facilitating hand washing and other personal hygiene activities. What am I? 2. I am a narrow ditch used to drain waste water from buildings to main gutters. I am open at the surface, you can see as waste water flows. What am I? 3. I am a container with a crucial role. Preventing littering and keeping the environment clean. In me, waste finds a temporary stay. Until proper disposal, I heed the call. What am I?\nLet us learn\n1. Waste bin: It is also known as a trash bin, garbage can or dustbin. It is a container specifically designed for the temporary storage and disposal of solid waste. Waste bins hold waste until it can be properly disposed of.\n2. Sink: This is a plumbing fixture that is fixed to the wall in a kitchen or bathroom. It consists of a basin with a tap for supplying water and a drain for removing waste water.\n3. Open drains: They are narrow channels or ditches designed to drain waste water or rain water away from buildings to main gutters by the roadsides. The main gutters then carry waste water out of the town. Open drains range from well-constructed ones made from cement, metals or plastic to simple trenches dug on the ground."},{"page":57,"pdfPage":61,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What are the consequences of not cleaning waste disposal facilities as shown in the following picture?","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p57-1.jpg","w":461,"h":354,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A learner covering his nose while walking past a dirty, blocked open drain."}]},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Explain the importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share your experiences on the importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Write down your ideas and present them in class."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"h5","text":"Importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Keeps away disease-causing germs to ensure good health and well-being of the family members."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Cleaning sinks and drains prevents clogs and blockages. This ensures proper flow of waste water."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Regular cleaning prevents bad odours."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Regular maintenance and cleaning can extend the lifespan of waste disposal facilities. This reduces the need for frequent repairs which helps to save money."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Clean waste disposal facilities contribute to comfortable and enjoyable living conditions."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Prevents build-up of dirt which may be expensive to remove."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a waste bin.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Waste bin, gloves, water, detergent, scrub brush or sponge, baking soda and trash bag.","indent":false}],"text":"Activity 4 Let us talk (in groups)\n1. What are the consequences of not cleaning waste disposal facilities as shown in the following picture?\nA learner covering his nose while walking past a dirty, blocked open drain.\n2. Explain the importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities.\n3. Share your experiences on the importance of cleaning waste disposal facilities.\n4. Write down your ideas and present them in class.\nLet us learn\nImportance of cleaning waste disposal facilities\n1. Keeps away disease-causing germs to ensure good health and well-being of the family members.\n2. Cleaning sinks and drains prevents clogs and blockages. This ensures proper flow of waste water.\n3. Regular cleaning prevents bad odours.\n4. Regular maintenance and cleaning can extend the lifespan of waste disposal facilities. This reduces the need for frequent repairs which helps to save money.\n5. Clean waste disposal facilities contribute to comfortable and enjoyable living conditions.\n6. Prevents build-up of dirt which may be expensive to remove.\nActivity 5 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a waste bin.\nRequirements\nWaste bin, gloves, water, detergent, scrub brush or sponge, baking soda and trash bag."},{"page":58,"pdfPage":62,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Collect the materials and equipment required."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with waste."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Empty the waste bin."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Use water to rinse the waste bin to remove loose dirt."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Fill a bucket with warm water and add the detergent."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Dip a sponge into the soapy water and scrub the interior and exterior of the waste bin properly, paying attention to the corners and the bottom of the bin. If there are odours or stains, sprinkle baking soda in the waste bin and let it sit for ten minutes before scrubbing it again.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p58-1.jpg","w":401,"h":338,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A gloved hand scrubbing the inside of a waste bin with a brush."}]},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the waste bin with clean warm water to remove soap and dirt."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Rinse with cold water to which disinfectant has been added."},{"n":"9.","level":1,"html":"Leave the bin to air dry thoroughly."},{"n":"10.","level":1,"html":"Clean, dry and store the tools, equipment and materials used for cleaning."},{"n":"11.","level":1,"html":"Once dry, line the waste bin ready for use.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p58-2.jpg","w":477,"h":368,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A clean waste bin lined with a bin liner."}]}]}],"text":"Steps to follow\n1. Collect the materials and equipment required.\n2. Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with waste.\n3. Empty the waste bin.\n4. Use water to rinse the waste bin to remove loose dirt.\n5. Fill a bucket with warm water and add the detergent.\n6. Dip a sponge into the soapy water and scrub the interior and exterior of the waste bin properly, paying attention to the corners and the bottom of the bin. If there are odours or stains, sprinkle baking soda in the waste bin and let it sit for ten minutes before scrubbing it again.\nA gloved hand scrubbing the inside of a waste bin with a brush.\n7. Rinse the waste bin with clean warm water to remove soap and dirt.\n8. Rinse with cold water to which disinfectant has been added.\n9. Leave the bin to air dry thoroughly.\n10. Clean, dry and store the tools, equipment and materials used for cleaning.\n11. Once dry, line the waste bin ready for use.\nA clean waste bin lined with a bin liner."},{"page":59,"pdfPage":63,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a sink.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Baking soda, gloves, scouring pad, soft clean cloth, clean toothbrush, hot water and vinegar.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Gather the necessary cleaning tools and materials."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Empty the sink and rinse it using clean water to remove loose dirt."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Sprinkle baking soda on the sink basin and let it sit for about 10 minutes.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p59-1.jpg","w":270,"h":249,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Baking soda being sprinkled into a sink."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Scrub the sink basin thoroughly. Clean the tap and under the sink using a scouring pad and warm soapy water."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Dip a clean toothbrush in the warm soapy water and use it to scrub the base of the tap and the drain.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p59-2.jpg","w":335,"h":283,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A gloved hand scrubbing the sink drain and tap with a brush."}]},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the sink using clean warm water followed by cold water."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Pour hot water mixed with vinegar down the drain to flush down the dirt, unclog and kill germs."},{"n":"9.","level":1,"html":"Allow cold water to run through the drain."},{"n":"10.","level":1,"html":"Dry the sink with a soft clean cloth."}]}],"text":"Activity 6 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a sink.\nRequirements\nBaking soda, gloves, scouring pad, soft clean cloth, clean toothbrush, hot water and vinegar.\nSteps to follow\n1. Gather the necessary cleaning tools and materials.\n2. Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt.\n3. Empty the sink and rinse it using clean water to remove loose dirt.\n4. Sprinkle baking soda on the sink basin and let it sit for about 10 minutes.\nBaking soda being sprinkled into a sink.\n5. Scrub the sink basin thoroughly. Clean the tap and under the sink using a scouring pad and warm soapy water.\n6. Dip a clean toothbrush in the warm soapy water and use it to scrub the base of the tap and the drain.\nA gloved hand scrubbing the sink drain and tap with a brush.\n7. Rinse the sink using clean warm water followed by cold water.\n8. Pour hot water mixed with vinegar down the drain to flush down the dirt, unclog and kill germs.\n9. Allow cold water to run through the drain.\n10. Dry the sink with a soft clean cloth."},{"page":60,"pdfPage":64,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"h5","text":"Note"},{"t":"p","html":"A clogged sink can allow build-up of dirt since waste water is not able to drain properly.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"If necessary, use the following procedure to unclog the sink:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Fill the sink halfway with cold water."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Position a suction pump over the drain.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p60-1.jpg","w":470,"h":465,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A gloved hand using a plunger (suction pump) on a clogged kitchen sink."}]},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Work the suction pump up and down quickly."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Remove the suction pump from the drain opening and see if the water starts to drain."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Continue using the suction pump until the clog is dislodged."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 7","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a cemented open drain.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Gloves, water, scrubbing brush, long stiff wire, detergent, bucket and disinfectant.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Steps to follow"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Collect the materials and equipment required."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Collect any litter or leaves from the drain so that waste water can flow easily."}]}],"text":"Note\nA clogged sink can allow build-up of dirt since waste water is not able to drain properly.\nIf necessary, use the following procedure to unclog the sink:\n1. Fill the sink halfway with cold water.\n2. Position a suction pump over the drain.\nA gloved hand using a plunger (suction pump) on a clogged kitchen sink.\n3. Work the suction pump up and down quickly.\n4. Remove the suction pump from the drain opening and see if the water starts to drain.\n5. Continue using the suction pump until the clog is dislodged.\nActivity 7 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean a cemented open drain.\nRequirements\nGloves, water, scrubbing brush, long stiff wire, detergent, bucket and disinfectant.\nSteps to follow\n1. Collect the materials and equipment required.\n2. Wear the gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt.\n3. Collect any litter or leaves from the drain so that waste water can flow easily."},{"page":61,"pdfPage":65,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Pour warm soapy water down the drain and scrub using a stiff bristle brush.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p61-1.jpg","w":375,"h":340,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A learner clearing litter from an open drain with a long-handled tool."}]},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Use clean warm water to rinse the drain."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"To eliminate odours, sprinkle baking soda over the drain surface. Follow this by pouring white vinegar over the baking soda."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Scrub using a brush. Flush the drain using clean hot water."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the drain using clean cold water."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 8","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean an earthen open drain.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Wear gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Collect any litter or leaves from the drains so that waste water can flow easily."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Pull out any weeds using your hands."}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Take home activity"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Assist your parent or guardian to clean waste disposal facilities at home."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Share your experiences in class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 9","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Decide how often the class waste bin needs to be cleaned."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Create a duty roster where each learner is assigned a day to clean the class waste bin."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Display the duty roster on the classroom noticeboard."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Ensure you all participate in cleaning the waste bin in order to create a healthy learning environment."}]}],"text":"4. Pour warm soapy water down the drain and scrub using a stiff bristle brush.\nA learner clearing litter from an open drain with a long-handled tool.\n5. Use clean warm water to rinse the drain.\n6. To eliminate odours, sprinkle baking soda over the drain surface. Follow this by pouring white vinegar over the baking soda.\n7. Scrub using a brush. Flush the drain using clean hot water.\n8. Rinse the drain using clean cold water.\nActivity 8 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, use the following procedure to clean an earthen open drain.\n1. Wear gloves to protect your hands from contact with dirt.\n2. Collect any litter or leaves from the drains so that waste water can flow easily.\n3. Pull out any weeds using your hands.\nTake home activity\n1. Assist your parent or guardian to clean waste disposal facilities at home.\n2. Share your experiences in class.\nActivity 9 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Decide how often the class waste bin needs to be cleaned.\n2. Create a duty roster where each learner is assigned a day to clean the class waste bin.\n3. Display the duty roster on the classroom noticeboard.\n4. Ensure you all participate in cleaning the waste bin in order to create a healthy learning environment."},{"page":62,"pdfPage":66,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.1","substrandTitle":"Cleaning Waste Disposal Facilities","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 10","label":"Let us do (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"Grade 9 learners are discussing some of the practices they will adopt to keep their waste disposal facilities clean. Read the presentations they made and answer the questions that follow.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p62-1.jpg","w":732,"h":409,"caption":"","labels":"Clean water should be poured through the drains after use. Use waste bin liners and change them regularly. Rinse the sink after each use."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What is the importance of pouring clean water through the drains after use?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Why is it recommended to rinse the sink after each use?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"What is the importance of lining waste bins?"}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Discuss the meaning of this statement: <strong>Regular cleaning is our first defence.</strong>"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Roman would like to assist his mother to clean their kitchen sink. However, the sink is clogged."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Which procedure should he use to unclog the sink?"},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Write down the procedure he is likely to use to clean the sink."}]}],"text":"Activity 10 Let us do (in pairs)\nGrade 9 learners are discussing some of the practices they will adopt to keep their waste disposal facilities clean. Read the presentations they made and answer the questions that follow.\nClean water should be poured through the drains after use. Use waste bin liners and change them regularly. Rinse the sink after each use.\n1. What is the importance of pouring clean water through the drains after use?\n2. Why is it recommended to rinse the sink after each use?\n3. What is the importance of lining waste bins?\nAssess yourself\n1. Discuss the meaning of this statement: Regular cleaning is our first defence.\n2. Roman would like to assist his mother to clean their kitchen sink. However, the sink is clogged.\na) Which procedure should he use to unclog the sink?\nb) Write down the procedure he is likely to use to clean the sink."},{"page":63,"pdfPage":67,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","code":"3.2"},{"t":"p","html":"Disinfecting is carried out to avoid the spread of communicable diseases from one person to another. Disinfecting reduces the chances of infection through clothing and household articles.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles"},{"t":"p","html":"Disinfection of clothing and household articles is important in maintaining cleanliness and promoting health within our homes. Disinfection can be done in various ways.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Share experiences on methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles you use at home.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"<strong>Study the pictures below and answer the questions that follow.</strong>","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p63-1.jpg","w":824,"h":460,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Picture a: a woman pouring a liquid into a basin of clothes. Picture b: a boy stirring clothes in a pot of boiling water on a cooker."},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Identify the methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles shown in pictures (a) and (b)."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Describe other methods used to disinfect clothing and household articles."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Share your ideas in class."}]}],"text":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles\nDisinfecting is carried out to avoid the spread of communicable diseases from one person to another. Disinfecting reduces the chances of infection through clothing and household articles.\nMethods of disinfecting clothing and household articles\nDisinfection of clothing and household articles is important in maintaining cleanliness and promoting health within our homes. Disinfection can be done in various ways.\nActivity 1 Let us talk (in groups)\nShare experiences on methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles you use at home.\nActivity 2 Let us do (in pairs)\nStudy the pictures below and answer the questions that follow.\nPicture a: a woman pouring a liquid into a basin of clothes. Picture b: a boy stirring clothes in a pot of boiling water on a cooker.\n1. Identify the methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles shown in pictures (a) and (b).\n2. Describe other methods used to disinfect clothing and household articles.\n3. Share your ideas in class."},{"page":64,"pdfPage":68,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us do (individually)"},{"t":"p","html":"Complete the following crossword puzzle on the methods used to disinfect clothing and household articles.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p64-1.jpg","w":538,"h":646,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Empty crossword grid with numbered spaces: 2, 3 and 5 across; 1 and 4 down."},{"t":"h5","text":"Across"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"This natural method uses UV rays to disinfect clothes when they are hung outdoors."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Which method involves heating water to a high temperature to disinfect clothes?"},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Which technique uses a common kitchen ingredient to help disinfect clothes?"}]},{"t":"h5","text":"Down"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Chemical agents specially formulated to kill germs on clothes are known as?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"A laundry process that not only removes wrinkles but also aids in disinfecting clothes. <strong>Hints</strong> Disinfectants, boiling, ironing, salting, sunlight"}]}],"text":"Activity 3 Let us do (individually)\nComplete the following crossword puzzle on the methods used to disinfect clothing and household articles.\nEmpty crossword grid with numbered spaces: 2, 3 and 5 across; 1 and 4 down.\nAcross\n2. This natural method uses UV rays to disinfect clothes when they are hung outdoors.\n3. Which method involves heating water to a high temperature to disinfect clothes?\n5. Which technique uses a common kitchen ingredient to help disinfect clothes?\nDown\n1. Chemical agents specially formulated to kill germs on clothes are known as?\n4. A laundry process that not only removes wrinkles but also aids in disinfecting clothes. Hints Disinfectants, boiling, ironing, salting, sunlight"},{"page":65,"pdfPage":69,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Boiling: This involves putting clothes which have been thoroughly washed and rinsed into boiling water. The high temperature helps to kill bacteria and viruses present in clothing and household articles."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Use of disinfectants: These are chemical substances used to kill disease-causing germs."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Sunlight: Sunlight is a natural disinfectant. Hanging clothes under the sun helps to kill viruses and bacteria."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Salting: This involves using table salt to soak clothes during the laundry process. This helps to kill disease-causing germs."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Ironing: This not only removes wrinkles from clothes but also disinfects. The heat generated by the iron helps to kill some microorganisms on the fabric. <strong>Note</strong>"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Methods of disinfecting clothing and household articles such as drying in the sun, salting and using a hot iron may be used to kill germs. However, they may not be effective by themselves. Therefore, you may consider using a combination of two methods to enhance the effectiveness.","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Disinfecting clothing and household articles"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"Which fabrics can withstand boiling as a method of disinfecting?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 5","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"h5","text":"Boiling"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, practise boiling as a method of disinfecting clothes.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Basins, soap, water, pegs, white cotton kitchen towel, clean stick or tongs, source of heat and <em>sufuria</em>.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Soak the kitchen towel in cold water for about 20 minutes to loosen dirt."}]}],"text":"Let us learn\n1. Boiling: This involves putting clothes which have been thoroughly washed and rinsed into boiling water. The high temperature helps to kill bacteria and viruses present in clothing and household articles.\n2. Use of disinfectants: These are chemical substances used to kill disease-causing germs.\n3. Sunlight: Sunlight is a natural disinfectant. Hanging clothes under the sun helps to kill viruses and bacteria.\n4. Salting: This involves using table salt to soak clothes during the laundry process. This helps to kill disease-causing germs.\n5. Ironing: This not only removes wrinkles from clothes but also disinfects. The heat generated by the iron helps to kill some microorganisms on the fabric. Note\nMethods of disinfecting clothing and household articles such as drying in the sun, salting and using a hot iron may be used to kill germs. However, they may not be effective by themselves. Therefore, you may consider using a combination of two methods to enhance the effectiveness.\nDisinfecting clothing and household articles\nActivity 4 Let us talk (in pairs)\nWhich fabrics can withstand boiling as a method of disinfecting?\nActivity 5 Let us do (in groups)\nBoiling\nWith the guidance of your teacher, practise boiling as a method of disinfecting clothes.\nRequirements\nBasins, soap, water, pegs, white cotton kitchen towel, clean stick or tongs, source of heat and sufuria.\nWhat to do\n1. Soak the kitchen towel in cold water for about 20 minutes to loosen dirt."},{"page":66,"pdfPage":70,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Wash the kitchen towel in warm soapy water using friction method. <em>(refer to</em> <em>the procedure of laundering a cotton item learnt in Grade 5).</em>"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the kitchen towel thoroughly to remove all traces of soap."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Put the kitchen towel in boiling water for about ten minutes."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Turn the kitchen towel from time to time using a clean stick or tongs.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p66-1.jpg","w":619,"h":548,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A kitchen towel being turned with a stick in a pot of boiling water."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Remove the kitchen towel from the boiling water. Make the final rinse in cold water to freshen."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Dry the kitchen towel on a clothesline under the sun. Secure with pegs."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"After drying, fold the kitchen towel and store in a dry place. <strong>Caution</strong>"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Observe safety precautions while boiling to prevent burns and scalds.","indent":false},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Boiling is suitable for white cottons and linen.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Occasionally turn clothing and household articles during boiling.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Clothing and household articles to be boiled must be washed and rinsed thoroughly before boiling.","indent":false}],"text":"2. Wash the kitchen towel in warm soapy water using friction method. (refer to the procedure of laundering a cotton item learnt in Grade 5).\n3. Rinse the kitchen towel thoroughly to remove all traces of soap.\n4. Put the kitchen towel in boiling water for about ten minutes.\n5. Turn the kitchen towel from time to time using a clean stick or tongs.\nA kitchen towel being turned with a stick in a pot of boiling water.\n6. Remove the kitchen towel from the boiling water. Make the final rinse in cold water to freshen.\n7. Dry the kitchen towel on a clothesline under the sun. Secure with pegs.\n8. After drying, fold the kitchen towel and store in a dry place. Caution\nObserve safety precautions while boiling to prevent burns and scalds.\nLet us learn\nBoiling is suitable for white cottons and linen.\nOccasionally turn clothing and household articles during boiling.\nClothing and household articles to be boiled must be washed and rinsed thoroughly before boiling."},{"page":67,"pdfPage":71,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 6","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, practise use of a disinfectant as a method of disinfecting clothes.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Basins, soap, water, pegs, socks, disinfectant, source of heat and <em>sufuria</em>.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Soak the socks in cold water for about 20 minutes to loosen dirt."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Remove the socks from the soaking water. Squeeze out excess water."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Wash the socks gently in the warm soapy water."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the socks in warm water. Rinse them again in cold water."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Add disinfectant to the final cold rinsing water and rinse the socks.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p67-1.jpg","w":510,"h":379,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A learner washing socks in a basin on a table outdoors."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Dry the socks on a clothesline. Secure them with pegs."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Remove them from the clothesline when dry."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Fold and store them in a drawer. <strong>Note</strong>"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Disinfectant can be put in:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"The soaking water: This is done when dealing with clothing or household articles that were exposed to infectious diseases."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Final rinsing water: Adding disinfectant to the final rinsing water ensures that any remaining bacteria or viruses are eliminated."}]}],"text":"Activity 6 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, practise use of a disinfectant as a method of disinfecting clothes.\nRequirements\nBasins, soap, water, pegs, socks, disinfectant, source of heat and sufuria.\nWhat to do\n1. Soak the socks in cold water for about 20 minutes to loosen dirt.\n2. Remove the socks from the soaking water. Squeeze out excess water.\n3. Wash the socks gently in the warm soapy water.\n4. Rinse the socks in warm water. Rinse them again in cold water.\n5. Add disinfectant to the final cold rinsing water and rinse the socks.\nA learner washing socks in a basin on a table outdoors.\n6. Dry the socks on a clothesline. Secure them with pegs.\n7. Remove them from the clothesline when dry.\n8. Fold and store them in a drawer. Note\nDisinfectant can be put in:\n1. The soaking water: This is done when dealing with clothing or household articles that were exposed to infectious diseases.\n2. Final rinsing water: Adding disinfectant to the final rinsing water ensures that any remaining bacteria or viruses are eliminated."},{"page":68,"pdfPage":72,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"Disinfectants are most suitable for fabrics that are damaged by boiling.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Carefully read and follow the instructions provided by the manufacturer on the disinfectant label.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Wear protective clothing such as gloves to avoid infections.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Some disinfectants may require rinsing after application. Follow the instructions to determine if rinsing is necessary. If so, follow the recommended rinsing process.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 7","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"With the guidance of your teacher, practise sun drying and ironing to disinfect an apron.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Basins, soap, water, pegs, white cotton apron, clothesline, ironing surface and iron box.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Soak the apron in cold water for 20 minutes."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Wash the apron in warm soapy water using friction method."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the apron in warm water to remove all traces of soap."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Rinse the apron in cold water to freshen it."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Dry the apron on a clothesline under the sun.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p68-1.jpg","w":468,"h":345,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"An apron drying on a clothesline in the sun."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Remove the apron from the clothesline while it is slightly damp."}]}],"text":"Let us learn\nDisinfectants are most suitable for fabrics that are damaged by boiling.\nCarefully read and follow the instructions provided by the manufacturer on the disinfectant label.\nWear protective clothing such as gloves to avoid infections.\nSome disinfectants may require rinsing after application. Follow the instructions to determine if rinsing is necessary. If so, follow the recommended rinsing process.\nActivity 7 Let us do (in groups)\nWith the guidance of your teacher, practise sun drying and ironing to disinfect an apron.\nRequirements\nBasins, soap, water, pegs, white cotton apron, clothesline, ironing surface and iron box.\nWhat to do\n1. Soak the apron in cold water for 20 minutes.\n2. Wash the apron in warm soapy water using friction method.\n3. Rinse the apron in warm water to remove all traces of soap.\n4. Rinse the apron in cold water to freshen it.\n5. Dry the apron on a clothesline under the sun.\nAn apron drying on a clothesline in the sun.\n6. Remove the apron from the clothesline while it is slightly damp."},{"page":69,"pdfPage":73,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Iron the apron with a hot iron to disinfect and remove creases.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p69-1.jpg","w":472,"h":343,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A learner ironing an apron on an ironing board."}]},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Air the apron and store it in a clean dry place ready for use."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 8","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Grade 9 learners researched on the internet for the procedure of disinfecting using the salting method. Read their findings.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Disinfecting using salting method"},{"t":"h5","text":"Requirements"},{"t":"p","html":"Basins, jug, table salt, tablespoon, soap, warm water, pegs, handkerchief and clothesline.","indent":true},{"t":"h5","text":"What to do"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Put a jug of warm water in a basin and add two tablespoons of table salt to the warm water."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Stir the salt in the warm water until it completely dissolves. This creates a salt solution with disinfectant properties."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Soak the handkerchief in the salty water, ensuring that it is fully immersed in the water."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Allow the handkerchief to soak for twenty minutes. This gives the salt enough time to work as a disinfectant."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Rub the handkerchief gently to remove mucus. Remove the handkerchief from the salty water."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Wash the handkerchief in warm soapy water. Rinse the handkerchief in warm water."},{"n":"7.","level":1,"html":"Make the final rinse in cold salty water to disinfect the handkerchief further."},{"n":"8.","level":1,"html":"Wring the handkerchief gently to remove excess water."},{"n":"9.","level":1,"html":"Hang the handkerchief on a clothesline and secure it with pegs."}]},{"t":"p","html":"Use the procedure to disinfect a handkerchief using salting.","indent":false}],"text":"7. Iron the apron with a hot iron to disinfect and remove creases.\nA learner ironing an apron on an ironing board.\n8. Air the apron and store it in a clean dry place ready for use.\nActivity 8 Let us do (in groups)\nGrade 9 learners researched on the internet for the procedure of disinfecting using the salting method. Read their findings.\nDisinfecting using salting method\nRequirements\nBasins, jug, table salt, tablespoon, soap, warm water, pegs, handkerchief and clothesline.\nWhat to do\n1. Put a jug of warm water in a basin and add two tablespoons of table salt to the warm water.\n2. Stir the salt in the warm water until it completely dissolves. This creates a salt solution with disinfectant properties.\n3. Soak the handkerchief in the salty water, ensuring that it is fully immersed in the water.\n4. Allow the handkerchief to soak for twenty minutes. This gives the salt enough time to work as a disinfectant.\n5. Rub the handkerchief gently to remove mucus. Remove the handkerchief from the salty water.\n6. Wash the handkerchief in warm soapy water. Rinse the handkerchief in warm water.\n7. Make the final rinse in cold salty water to disinfect the handkerchief further.\n8. Wring the handkerchief gently to remove excess water.\n9. Hang the handkerchief on a clothesline and secure it with pegs.\nUse the procedure to disinfect a handkerchief using salting."},{"page":70,"pdfPage":74,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of disinfecting clothing and household articles"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 9","label":"Let us talk (in pairs)"},{"t":"p","html":"Lilian's family is caring for their grandmother, Mrs Kate, who is suffering from an infectious disease. Lilian has observed that her grandmother's clothing, bedding and towels are laundered separately. They are also thoroughly disinfected.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Why do you think it is important for Mrs Kate's clothing, towels and bedding to be disinfected?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Share your points in class."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 10","label":"Let us debate"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Organise the classroom for a debate."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Debate on the following topic: <strong>Regular disinfection of clothing and household</strong> <strong>articles is necessary for maintaining a healthy living environment.</strong>"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Record the presentations made during the debate."}]},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 11","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Make posters on the importance of disinfecting personal items (handkerchiefs, socks, towels, gloves, aprons and dustcoats)."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Display the posters on the school noticeboard to create awareness."}]},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"The following are the benefits of disinfecting clothing and household articles:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Prevents infections: Disinfecting clothes and household articles helps to kill germs, which reduces the risk of spreading communicable diseases."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Promotes personal hygiene: Disinfecting clothes and household articles is crucial in enhancing personal hygiene."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Prevents cross contamination: Disinfecting clothing is important after exposure to areas that may be contaminated such as hospitals or public transportation, to minimise the risk of carrying viruses and bacteria into the home."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Special groups of people may be vulnerable to infections. Therefore, disinfecting clothing and household articles is crucial in households with special groups of people to protect their health. Special groups of people include expectant women, children, the elderly and the sick."}]}],"text":"Importance of disinfecting clothing and household articles\nActivity 9 Let us talk (in pairs)\nLilian's family is caring for their grandmother, Mrs Kate, who is suffering from an infectious disease. Lilian has observed that her grandmother's clothing, bedding and towels are laundered separately. They are also thoroughly disinfected.\n1. Why do you think it is important for Mrs Kate's clothing, towels and bedding to be disinfected?\n2. Share your points in class.\nActivity 10 Let us debate\n1. Organise the classroom for a debate.\n2. Debate on the following topic: Regular disinfection of clothing and household articles is necessary for maintaining a healthy living environment.\n3. Record the presentations made during the debate.\nActivity 11 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Make posters on the importance of disinfecting personal items (handkerchiefs, socks, towels, gloves, aprons and dustcoats).\n2. Display the posters on the school noticeboard to create awareness.\nLet us learn\nThe following are the benefits of disinfecting clothing and household articles:\n1. Prevents infections: Disinfecting clothes and household articles helps to kill germs, which reduces the risk of spreading communicable diseases.\n2. Promotes personal hygiene: Disinfecting clothes and household articles is crucial in enhancing personal hygiene.\n3. Prevents cross contamination: Disinfecting clothing is important after exposure to areas that may be contaminated such as hospitals or public transportation, to minimise the risk of carrying viruses and bacteria into the home.\n4. Special groups of people may be vulnerable to infections. Therefore, disinfecting clothing and household articles is crucial in households with special groups of people to protect their health. Special groups of people include expectant women, children, the elderly and the sick."},{"page":71,"pdfPage":75,"strand":"3.0","strandTitle":"Hygiene Practices","substrand":"3.2","substrandTitle":"Disinfecting Clothing and Household Articles","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Grade 9 learners are making a presentation to educate members of their community on disinfecting clothing and household articles. Explain the methods of disinfecting they are likely to include in their presentation."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"You have been tasked to disinfect a cotton towel of a person suffering from a highly infectious skin disease."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Which method of disinfecting would you use? Explain your reasons."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Which safety precautions would you observe when disinfecting the towel?"},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"Write down the procedure of the disinfection method you chose."}]}],"text":"Assess yourself\n1. Grade 9 learners are making a presentation to educate members of their community on disinfecting clothing and household articles. Explain the methods of disinfecting they are likely to include in their presentation.\n2. You have been tasked to disinfect a cotton towel of a person suffering from a highly infectious skin disease.\na) Which method of disinfecting would you use? Explain your reasons.\nb) Which safety precautions would you observe when disinfecting the towel?\nc) Write down the procedure of the disinfection method you chose."},{"page":72,"pdfPage":76,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.1","substrandTitle":"Grafting in Plants","blocks":[{"t":"strand","text":"Production Techniques","code":"4.0"},{"t":"p","html":"In this strand, we will learn how to carry out grafting. We will also construct a homemade sun dryer for preserving vegetables.","indent":false},{"t":"substrand","text":"Grafting in Plants","code":"4.1"},{"t":"p","html":"Grafting is a technique used in crop production. Grafting is done by joining two parts of closely related plants so that they grow as one plant. When they are joined, the plant is called a grafted plant. The upper part of the graft is called a scion while the lower part is called a rootstock. Grafting can be done on crops such as fruit plants and also on flowers.","indent":false},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p72-1.jpg","w":321,"h":318,"caption":"Parts of a grafted plant","labels":"scion rootstock"},{"t":"figure","src":"media/book-figures/p72-2.jpg","w":419,"h":319,"caption":"A grafted mango seedling","labels":"graft union"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us go digital"},{"t":"p","html":"Use digital devices or books and magazines to search for examples of plants that can be grafted. In your search, observe the parts that are cut from each plant and how they are tied together to form one plant. Then, discuss the following questions.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which are examples of plants that can be grafted?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"What could have been the main reason for grafting the plants that you have studied?"}]}],"text":"Production Techniques\nIn this strand, we will learn how to carry out grafting. We will also construct a homemade sun dryer for preserving vegetables.\nGrafting in Plants\nGrafting is a technique used in crop production. Grafting is done by joining two parts of closely related plants so that they grow as one plant. When they are joined, the plant is called a grafted plant. The upper part of the graft is called a scion while the lower part is called a rootstock. Grafting can be done on crops such as fruit plants and also on flowers.\nParts of a grafted plant scion rootstock\nA grafted mango seedling graft union\nActivity 1 Let us go digital\nUse digital devices or books and magazines to search for examples of plants that can be grafted. In your search, observe the parts that are cut from each plant and how they are tied together to form one plant. Then, discuss the following questions.\n1. Which are examples of plants that can be grafted?\n2. What could have been the main reason for grafting the plants that you have studied?"},{"page":73,"pdfPage":77,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.1","substrandTitle":"Grafting in Plants","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Carrying out grafting for various reasons"},{"t":"p","html":"Grafting may be done in crops such as fruits and also on flowers. On flowers such as rose and bougainvillea, it can be done to produce more than one variety of flower colour. On fruit plants, it can be done to ensure that they mature faster by obtaining the scion from a fast maturing food crop. It can also be done to get improved varieties of fruits, to produce a fruit plant that can resist some diseases or a plant that can tolerate a harsh environment. Grafting can also be done to repair a damaged plant or to rejuvenate <em>(renew growth of)</em> an old plant.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Carrying out grafting"},{"t":"p","html":"How is grafting carried out?","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Search for plants such as rose flowers, bougainvillea, mango, orange, lemon, lime, avocado or any other plants of your choice. Your teacher will guide you to determine plants that can successfully be grafted and explain to you the main reason for grafting the selected plants.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Graft the plant by doing the following:"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Select a healthy plant that has the qualities you desire, for example, a mango tree that produces sweet tasting mango fruits. You will cut the scion from the selected plant."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Select a healthy growing seedling to be your rootstock, for example, a vigorously growing seedling that grows well in your local area."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Select a branch from the desired plant. Cut a small branch that has some nodes or buds that can produce leaves. It should match the diameter of the rootstock seedling."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Make a sharp V-shaped cut at the top of the rootstock seedling and a perfectly fitting shape on the base of the scion as shown in the pictures below.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p73-1.jpg","w":345,"h":277,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"Two drawings of a scion and a rootstock: the top of the rootstock has a V-shaped cut and the base of the scion is cut to a matching wedge, shown before and after they are fitted together."}]}]}],"text":"Carrying out grafting for various reasons\nGrafting may be done in crops such as fruits and also on flowers. On flowers such as rose and bougainvillea, it can be done to produce more than one variety of flower colour. On fruit plants, it can be done to ensure that they mature faster by obtaining the scion from a fast maturing food crop. It can also be done to get improved varieties of fruits, to produce a fruit plant that can resist some diseases or a plant that can tolerate a harsh environment. Grafting can also be done to repair a damaged plant or to rejuvenate (renew growth of) an old plant.\nCarrying out grafting\nHow is grafting carried out?\nActivity 2 Let us do (in groups)\nSearch for plants such as rose flowers, bougainvillea, mango, orange, lemon, lime, avocado or any other plants of your choice. Your teacher will guide you to determine plants that can successfully be grafted and explain to you the main reason for grafting the selected plants.\nGraft the plant by doing the following:\n1. Select a healthy plant that has the qualities you desire, for example, a mango tree that produces sweet tasting mango fruits. You will cut the scion from the selected plant.\n2. Select a healthy growing seedling to be your rootstock, for example, a vigorously growing seedling that grows well in your local area.\n3. Select a branch from the desired plant. Cut a small branch that has some nodes or buds that can produce leaves. It should match the diameter of the rootstock seedling.\n4. Make a sharp V-shaped cut at the top of the rootstock seedling and a perfectly fitting shape on the base of the scion as shown in the pictures below.\nTwo drawings of a scion and a rootstock: the top of the rootstock has a V-shaped cut and the base of the scion is cut to a matching wedge, shown before and after they are fitted together."},{"page":74,"pdfPage":78,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.1","substrandTitle":"Grafting in Plants","blocks":[{"t":"p","html":"While cutting the scion and rootstock, use a sharp tool to achieve a smooth cut. Take care of your fingers when cutting to avoid injuries.","indent":true},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Insert the scion into the rootstock and tie the union tightly. Seal the union using wax or any other suitable sealant to prevent entry of water as shown below.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p74-1.jpg","w":488,"h":380,"caption":"","labels":"","alt":"A grafted seedling with the graft union tied tightly with grafting tape."}]},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Wrap the graft union using grafting tape to further secure the union from water. Entry of water in the graft union leads to rotting, hence death of the scion."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Caring for the grafted seedling"},{"t":"p","html":"A grafted plant requires care to ensure that the graft union grows successfully. If the plant is neglected, the scion dies and falls off from the rootstock.","indent":false},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 3","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Carry out the following activities to ensure that the grafted plant grows successfully.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Protecting the plant: Construct a structure that protects the grafted plant from interference. Avoid touching the graft union to prevent weakening the union."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Watering the plant: Irrigate the plant regularly to prevent water stress. This ensures the plant system supplies nutrients to the growing scion for leaves to grow from the buds. Ensure that you do not splash water on the graft union."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Removing other buds: Remove any other buds that may grow below the graft union (in the rootstock). This ensures that only the desired branch grows on the plant."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Removal of graft tape: Once the buds have grown into leaves and the graft union is fully healed, remove the graft tape to allow the scion to grow wider and stronger."}]}],"text":"While cutting the scion and rootstock, use a sharp tool to achieve a smooth cut. Take care of your fingers when cutting to avoid injuries.\n5. Insert the scion into the rootstock and tie the union tightly. Seal the union using wax or any other suitable sealant to prevent entry of water as shown below.\nA grafted seedling with the graft union tied tightly with grafting tape.\n6. Wrap the graft union using grafting tape to further secure the union from water. Entry of water in the graft union leads to rotting, hence death of the scion.\nCaring for the grafted seedling\nA grafted plant requires care to ensure that the graft union grows successfully. If the plant is neglected, the scion dies and falls off from the rootstock.\nActivity 3 Let us do (in groups)\nCarry out the following activities to ensure that the grafted plant grows successfully.\n1. Protecting the plant: Construct a structure that protects the grafted plant from interference. Avoid touching the graft union to prevent weakening the union.\n2. Watering the plant: Irrigate the plant regularly to prevent water stress. This ensures the plant system supplies nutrients to the growing scion for leaves to grow from the buds. Ensure that you do not splash water on the graft union.\n3. Removing other buds: Remove any other buds that may grow below the graft union (in the rootstock). This ensures that only the desired branch grows on the plant.\n4. Removal of graft tape: Once the buds have grown into leaves and the graft union is fully healed, remove the graft tape to allow the scion to grow wider and stronger."},{"page":75,"pdfPage":79,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.1","substrandTitle":"Grafting in Plants","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 4","label":"Let us talk (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Discuss the plants that you can graft for the following reasons."},{"n":"a)","level":2,"html":"Beauty at the homestead."},{"n":"b)","level":2,"html":"Fruit production at home."},{"n":"c)","level":2,"html":"For sale to your neighbours."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Your sister, Jane, who is in Grade 4, planted an orange seedling in the school compound. One day, a Grade 1 boy unintentionally stepped on the young seedling and broke it into two pieces. Jane caught up with the young boy and reported him to you. Discuss how you would resolve the conflict and ensure that Jane's orange seedling project continues."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Importance of grafting plants"},{"t":"learn"},{"t":"p","html":"In the previous activities, we have learnt that grafting can be done for the following reasons:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"To get the desired quality of fruits."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"To grow a single plant that produces a variety of fruits or flowers for beauty purposes. This increases the aesthetic value of plants in the homestead."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"To repair damaged seedlings or plants."}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"State five reasons for grafting plants."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Explain how you can repair a damaged mango seedling."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Your neighbour has a new variety of colourful rose flower plants. Your parents have asked you to establish a new variety of rose flower to replace an old white rose flower in the homestead. Explain how you can help your parents to achieve this purpose."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Name the parts of a grafted avocado seedling described by the following statements:"},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"The upper part of the grafted seedling."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"The lower part of the grafted seedling."},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"The place that is tied with grafting tape to join and seal the grafted seedling."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Outline four practices carried out in caring for a grafted seedling to ensure successful growth of the plant."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"What care should one take when carrying out grafting to ensure safety from sharp grafting tools?"}]}],"text":"Activity 4 Let us talk (in groups)\n1. Discuss the plants that you can graft for the following reasons.\na) Beauty at the homestead.\nb) Fruit production at home.\nc) For sale to your neighbours.\n2. Your sister, Jane, who is in Grade 4, planted an orange seedling in the school compound. One day, a Grade 1 boy unintentionally stepped on the young seedling and broke it into two pieces. Jane caught up with the young boy and reported him to you. Discuss how you would resolve the conflict and ensure that Jane's orange seedling project continues.\nImportance of grafting plants\nLet us learn\nIn the previous activities, we have learnt that grafting can be done for the following reasons:\n1. To get the desired quality of fruits.\n2. To grow a single plant that produces a variety of fruits or flowers for beauty purposes. This increases the aesthetic value of plants in the homestead.\n3. To repair damaged seedlings or plants.\nAssess yourself\n1. State five reasons for grafting plants.\n2. Explain how you can repair a damaged mango seedling.\n3. Your neighbour has a new variety of colourful rose flower plants. Your parents have asked you to establish a new variety of rose flower to replace an old white rose flower in the homestead. Explain how you can help your parents to achieve this purpose.\n4. Name the parts of a grafted avocado seedling described by the following statements:\na. The upper part of the grafted seedling.\nb. The lower part of the grafted seedling.\nc. The place that is tied with grafting tape to join and seal the grafted seedling.\n5. Outline four practices carried out in caring for a grafted seedling to ensure successful growth of the plant.\n6. What care should one take when carrying out grafting to ensure safety from sharp grafting tools?"},{"page":76,"pdfPage":80,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.2","substrandTitle":"Homemade Sun Dryer","blocks":[{"t":"substrand","text":"Homemade Sun Dryer","code":"4.2"},{"t":"p","html":"Vegetables are important sources of nutrients that should be included in every meal. We should therefore grow vegetables at home to ensure their availability in our meals. During the rainy seasons, some vegetables grow in plenty. However, many vegetables, especially the green leafy ones have a short storage life. We can preserve the vegetables to be used throughout the year by the sun-drying method. Drying the vegetables for preservation requires the use of a sun dryer in order to retain the quality of vegetables. How can we make a sun dryer to dry the vegetables for preservation?","indent":false},{"t":"h3","text":"Searching for information on a homemade sun dryer"},{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 1","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"p","html":"Use books, magazines, digital devices or enquire from a knowledgeable person how to make a homemade sun dryer for vegetables. In your search for information, observe the parts of a homemade sun dryer, the materials used to make it and the appropriate size that one can make using locally available materials. Then, discuss the following questions.","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Which vegetables can you dry for preservation using a homemade sun dryer?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Which materials can you use to construct a homemade sun dryer for vegetables?"},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"What structure do you require to construct the sun dryer?"},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Why should households dry vegetables using a homemade sun dryer?"}]},{"t":"h3","text":"Construction of a homemade sun dryer for vegetable preservation"},{"t":"p","html":"A homemade sun dryer can be made using locally available materials. All that is required is an understanding of how to dry the green vegetables without over-exposing the vegetables to direct sunlight in order to conserve most of their nutrients, colour and taste. The locally available materials can be acquired from the remains of construction materials used for other purposes at home or in school. The design of the structure requires a frame structure with the shape of your choice and enclosing materials such as polythene sheet or glass to allow passage of sunlight. The sunlight brings in heat energy from the sun to remove excess moisture from the vegetables. A holding tray can be constructed using wire mesh to hold the vegetables inside the structure and allow free circulation of heat energy while drying the vegetables.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Before starting the construction, make a sketch of the homemade sun dryer. Use the ideas that you have gained so far. List the materials that you can use based on what is available in your environment.","indent":false}],"text":"Homemade Sun Dryer\nVegetables are important sources of nutrients that should be included in every meal. We should therefore grow vegetables at home to ensure their availability in our meals. During the rainy seasons, some vegetables grow in plenty. However, many vegetables, especially the green leafy ones have a short storage life. We can preserve the vegetables to be used throughout the year by the sun-drying method. Drying the vegetables for preservation requires the use of a sun dryer in order to retain the quality of vegetables. How can we make a sun dryer to dry the vegetables for preservation?\nSearching for information on a homemade sun dryer\nActivity 1 Let us do (in groups)\nUse books, magazines, digital devices or enquire from a knowledgeable person how to make a homemade sun dryer for vegetables. In your search for information, observe the parts of a homemade sun dryer, the materials used to make it and the appropriate size that one can make using locally available materials. Then, discuss the following questions.\n1. Which vegetables can you dry for preservation using a homemade sun dryer?\n2. Which materials can you use to construct a homemade sun dryer for vegetables?\n3. What structure do you require to construct the sun dryer?\n4. Why should households dry vegetables using a homemade sun dryer?\nConstruction of a homemade sun dryer for vegetable preservation\nA homemade sun dryer can be made using locally available materials. All that is required is an understanding of how to dry the green vegetables without over-exposing the vegetables to direct sunlight in order to conserve most of their nutrients, colour and taste. The locally available materials can be acquired from the remains of construction materials used for other purposes at home or in school. The design of the structure requires a frame structure with the shape of your choice and enclosing materials such as polythene sheet or glass to allow passage of sunlight. The sunlight brings in heat energy from the sun to remove excess moisture from the vegetables. A holding tray can be constructed using wire mesh to hold the vegetables inside the structure and allow free circulation of heat energy while drying the vegetables.\nBefore starting the construction, make a sketch of the homemade sun dryer. Use the ideas that you have gained so far. List the materials that you can use based on what is available in your environment."},{"page":77,"pdfPage":81,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.2","substrandTitle":"Homemade Sun Dryer","blocks":[{"t":"activity","num":"Activity 2","label":"Let us do (in groups)"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Sketch and construct a supportive frame structure using pieces of wood or strong wires to form the shape of your choice. The size depends on the quantity of vegetables you would like to preserve at once."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Construct a vegetable holding tray using a screen mesh and install it inside the frame structure. You can make this using a wire net or any other netting material. The number of trays depends on the size of your structure."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Cover the frame structure using a material that allows passage of sunlight. This part can be made using a clear polythene sheet. The enclosed structure should form a small cage that concentrates sunlight inside it so as to work like a greenhouse structure."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Place the constructed sun dryer in an exposed area that receives adequate sunlight throughout the day. Place a thermometer on the tray inside the sun dryer. Observe the temperature inside the sun dryer after a period of about one hour.","figures":[{"src":"media/book-figures/p77-1.jpg","w":746,"h":498,"caption":"Homemade sun dryer","labels":""}]}]},{"t":"p","html":"You can determine whether the sun dryer is functioning properly by comparing the external temperature with the internal temperature (temperature inside the sun dryer) after placing the thermometer on the tray for a period of time. Alternatively, you can weigh the vegetables before putting them in the sun dryer and after putting them inside for some time and compare the weights.","indent":false}],"text":"Activity 2 Let us do (in groups)\n1. Sketch and construct a supportive frame structure using pieces of wood or strong wires to form the shape of your choice. The size depends on the quantity of vegetables you would like to preserve at once.\n2. Construct a vegetable holding tray using a screen mesh and install it inside the frame structure. You can make this using a wire net or any other netting material. The number of trays depends on the size of your structure.\n3. Cover the frame structure using a material that allows passage of sunlight. This part can be made using a clear polythene sheet. The enclosed structure should form a small cage that concentrates sunlight inside it so as to work like a greenhouse structure.\n4. Place the constructed sun dryer in an exposed area that receives adequate sunlight throughout the day. Place a thermometer on the tray inside the sun dryer. Observe the temperature inside the sun dryer after a period of about one hour.\nHomemade sun dryer\nYou can determine whether the sun dryer is functioning properly by comparing the external temperature with the internal temperature (temperature inside the sun dryer) after placing the thermometer on the tray for a period of time. Alternatively, you can weigh the vegetables before putting them in the sun dryer and after putting them inside for some time and compare the weights."},{"page":78,"pdfPage":82,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.2","substrandTitle":"Homemade Sun Dryer","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Using the sun dryer to dry vegetables for preservation"},{"t":"p","html":"Once the sun dryer has been constructed and tested, it can be used to dry green vegetables for preservation purposes. To use the dryer:","indent":false},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"Clean the constructed dryer by wiping it with clean soapy water and rinse it by wiping with a rinsed moist piece of cloth. Remember to clean the holding tray as well."},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Place the sun dryer in a suitable clean environment with adequate exposure to sunlight throughout the day."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"Take some clean dry leafy vegetables and arrange them on the tray inside the dryer. Ensure they are arranged in a single layer and evenly spread to allow adequate air circulation."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Close the sun dryer's entry (door) to trap the heat inside the sun dryer."},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Check the drying vegetables after a few hours and turn them to ensure they dry evenly. The drying period may vary from a few days to a week depending on the weather conditions and the amount of moisture in the vegetables."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Remove the vegetables from the sun dryer once they are dried and put them in a tightly closed container for preservation. <strong>Caution</strong>"}]},{"t":"p","html":"Observe personal hygiene when handling the vegetables.","indent":false},{"t":"p","html":"Ensure the vegetables put in the sun dryer are clean and free from contamination. When it is very cold, especially at night, put the sun dryer inside a room to protect your vegetables.","indent":false},{"t":"h5","text":"Take Home Activity"},{"t":"p","html":"After learning how to construct and use a homemade sun dryer, you can make a sun dryer of a size that suits your home use. You can seek your parent's or guardian's support in acquiring available materials and constructing a sun dryer at home to be used by your family. This way, you will be a responsible person who can help your family members and friends to enhance food security at family level.","indent":false}],"text":"Using the sun dryer to dry vegetables for preservation\nOnce the sun dryer has been constructed and tested, it can be used to dry green vegetables for preservation purposes. To use the dryer:\n1. Clean the constructed dryer by wiping it with clean soapy water and rinse it by wiping with a rinsed moist piece of cloth. Remember to clean the holding tray as well.\n2. Place the sun dryer in a suitable clean environment with adequate exposure to sunlight throughout the day.\n3. Take some clean dry leafy vegetables and arrange them on the tray inside the dryer. Ensure they are arranged in a single layer and evenly spread to allow adequate air circulation.\n4. Close the sun dryer's entry (door) to trap the heat inside the sun dryer.\n5. Check the drying vegetables after a few hours and turn them to ensure they dry evenly. The drying period may vary from a few days to a week depending on the weather conditions and the amount of moisture in the vegetables.\n6. Remove the vegetables from the sun dryer once they are dried and put them in a tightly closed container for preservation. Caution\nObserve personal hygiene when handling the vegetables.\nEnsure the vegetables put in the sun dryer are clean and free from contamination. When it is very cold, especially at night, put the sun dryer inside a room to protect your vegetables.\nTake Home Activity\nAfter learning how to construct and use a homemade sun dryer, you can make a sun dryer of a size that suits your home use. You can seek your parent's or guardian's support in acquiring available materials and constructing a sun dryer at home to be used by your family. This way, you will be a responsible person who can help your family members and friends to enhance food security at family level."},{"page":79,"pdfPage":83,"strand":"4.0","strandTitle":"Production Techniques","substrand":"4.2","substrandTitle":"Homemade Sun Dryer","blocks":[{"t":"h3","text":"Assess yourself"},{"t":"list","items":[{"n":"1.","level":1,"html":"What is a vegetable sun dryer?"},{"n":"2.","level":1,"html":"Some vegetables are often in surplus during the rainy season."},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"Explain the use of a sun dryer in enhancing food security."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"Apart from ensuring food security, give other reasons why the use of homemade sun dryers should be adopted in our homes."},{"n":"3.","level":1,"html":"List four leafy vegetables that can be dried for preservation."},{"n":"4.","level":1,"html":"Name two materials that can be used to construct the following parts of a sun dryer for vegetables."},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"The frame structure: _________________ or ___________________"},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"The tray: ________________________ or _____________________"},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"The dryer covering: ______________________ or _______________"},{"n":"5.","level":1,"html":"Give two reasons for each of the following parts of a sun dryer"},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"The transparent covering."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"The tray."},{"n":"6.","level":1,"html":"Explain the importance of the following practices in sun drying vegetables."},{"n":"a.","level":2,"html":"Cleaning the dryer."},{"n":"b.","level":2,"html":"Cleaning the vegetables."},{"n":"c.","level":2,"html":"Placing the dryer in direct sunlight."}]}],"text":"Assess yourself\n1. What is a vegetable sun dryer?\n2. Some vegetables are often in surplus during the rainy season.\na. Explain the use of a sun dryer in enhancing food security.\nb. Apart from ensuring food security, give other reasons why the use of homemade sun dryers should be adopted in our homes.\n3. List four leafy vegetables that can be dried for preservation.\n4. Name two materials that can be used to construct the following parts of a sun dryer for vegetables.\na. The frame structure: _________________ or ___________________\nb. The tray: ________________________ or _____________________\nc. The dryer covering: ______________________ or _______________\n5. Give two reasons for each of the following parts of a sun dryer\na. The transparent covering.\nb. The tray.\n6. Explain the importance of the following practices in sun drying vegetables.\na. Cleaning the dryer.\nb. Cleaning the vegetables.\nc. Placing the dryer in direct sunlight."}]};
